  ('Liang claims maiden ranking title', 'liang-claims-maiden-ranking-title', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Liang Wenbo became the inaugural winner of the Coral English Open by beating Judd Trump 9-6 in the final in Manchester, winning his first ranking title.', null, 'Trump’s fight-back continued as a run of 50 drew him within one frame. And he led 41-7 in frame 14, only for Liang to make a tremendous 62 clearance from an unpromising initial position, which moved him within one of victory at 8-6.', 'Liang Wenbo became the inaugural winner of the Coral English Open by beating Judd Trump 9-6 in the final in Manchester, winning his first ranking title.

more Liang led throughout the final, and fended off a late rally from Trump, the crucial moment coming in the 14th frame when he made a brilliant clearance to go 8-6 ahead. Soon afterwards the player nicknamed The Firecracker was exploding with exuberant celebrations as he potted the winning balls, before lifting the new Steve Davis Trophy and pocketing a cheque for £70,000. The 29-year-old could be in line for a far bigger prize later in the season as victory in the other three Home Nations events – the Northern Ireland Open, Scottish Open and Welsh Open – would land him a massive £1 million bonus. But for now Liang can celebrate becoming the only player from mainland China other than Ding Junhui to win a world ranking title. His previous career highlights include winning an Asian Tour event in 2013 and the World Cup for China alongside Ding in 2011. But this is clearly his best moment on the baize and he’s rewarded with a leap of eight places in the world rankings to number 12, as well as a place in next month’s Champion of Champions.in Coventry. Liang’s two previous ranking finals were the 2009 Shanghai Masters when he lost 10-5 to Ronnie O’Sullivan and last year’s UK Championship when he was beaten by Neil Robertson by the same scoreline. This time he faced another of snooker’s top stars, and one who had won his last 14 consecutive matches, but this time Liang rose to the occasion with an excellent performance. Trump, having won last week’s European Masters, missed out on the chance to become the 11th player in snooker history to win back to back ranking titles. He receives £30,000 and remains third in the world. Liang led 5-3 after the first session having compiled breaks of 95, 95, 76 and 92. Bristol’s 27-year-old Trump started the evening session strongly with a break of 132, but Liang hit back with 84 and 98 to lead 7-4. Frame 12 came down to the last two reds, and a missed attempt at a long pot from Liang allowed Trump to close the gap to 7-5 at the interval. Trump’s fight-back continued as a run of 50 drew him within one frame. And he led 41-7 in frame 14, only for Liang to make a tremendous 62 clearance from an unpromising initial position, which moved him within one of victory at 8-6. Liang had two early scoring chances in the 15th frame but missed one red to a top corner with the rest and another to a baulk corner. Trump made 46 to lead by 15 points before leaving a tricky red along the top cushion in the jaws of a pocket. And that proved his last shot as Liang held his nerve to make a match-winning 39, jumping high in delight as each of the last few colours went down. “I’m very happy,” said Liang. “In the last two frames I concentrated on every shot and made two good breaks. Snooker is a very difficult game but if you don’t enjoy it you don’t play well. Judd put me under pressure but to go 8-6 ahead gave me more confidence. “I have to say thank you to Ronnie O’Sullivan because I practise with him and eat with him every day. I have learned a lot from him over the past two years and he’s my best friend. Yesterday he told me to look back at my UK Championship final last year and see what I could learn from it. I knew I needed to be more calm and controlled this time. “I’m going to forget about this victory now and keep going in the next one. I always learn from every experience.” Liang also mentioned a chance meeting with legendary football manager Sir Alex Ferguson in his hotel yesterday, adding: “He said he would be watching my matches and he told me to concentrate.” Trump, who remains stuck on six career ranking titles, from 12 finals, said: “I was chasing him throughout the day but I should have made it 7-7 and if I had done that it might have been a different result. I should have turned the screw and grabbed control of the match. I felt a bit of pressure and I was taking too much time, when I should have just got on with it. “Liang was playing with freedom and his long potting was unbelievable. It’s good to have another winner from China. “I’m annoyed not to get back-to-back ranking titles but winning 14 matches in a row has given me confidence and I will try to start a new run now. When you lose a final it gives you another kick because you don’t want to feel like that again. It’s a good time to be in form with so many tournaments coming up.” Article & Images courtesy of the World Snooker', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Life Member & former Secretary for nearly 30 years Gordon Boynton sadly passes away...', 'life-member-former-secretary-for-nearly-30-years-gordon-boynton-sadly-passes-awa', 'League News', '[]'::jsonb, 'It is with much regret that we have to report that St Blazey and District Snooker League Life Member and former Secretary for nearly 30 years Gordon Boynton has passed away. His funeral is to be held at Glyn Valley…', null, null, 'It is with much regret that we have to report that St Blazey and District Snooker League Life Member and former Secretary for nearly 30 years Gordon Boynton has passed away. His funeral is to be held at Glyn Valley Crematorium at 2.30 p.m on Tuesday 21st August.', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Life without Ronnie', 'life-without-ronnie', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'This week sees the start of the new Premier League. A stellar line-up includes John Higgins, Judd Trump, Stuart Bingham, Peter Ebdon, Ding Junhui, Neil Robertson, Mark Allen, Mark Selby, Stephen Lee and Shaun Murphy.', null, '"O''Sullivan is by a distance the most innately talented player in the history of the baize, the biggest draw at a time when snooker needs all the drawing power it can muster"', 'This week sees the start of the new Premier League. A stellar line-up includes John Higgins, Judd Trump, Stuart Bingham, Peter Ebdon, Ding Junhui, Neil Robertson, Mark Allen, Mark Selby, Stephen Lee and Shaun Murphy.

more But reigning champion Ronnie O''Sullivan is missing. The four-time world champion has claimed this invitational event as his own with ten titles and an impressive seven in the last eight years. His absence will without doubt take away something from the tournament as it travels around the country but lead referee, Paul Collier, who returns for another season to write exclusive columns for OnCue, says the show must go on...

LEARNING TO DEAL WITH LIFE

Ronnie''s absence is going to be a little detrimental to the Premier League, of course, but no-one is bigger than the game. Tickets will probably sell a little less quickly because he''s such a box office attraction, but I''m not worried about the event overall this season. It''s a shame he isn''t going to be in it. He''s the defending champion and also the world champion. Any event would suffer from him not being there. He''s a massive character and a great player. I''ve got to know Ronnie fairly well over the last few years and I hope he''s in a happier place now after his break. Maybe he''ll be back with us next year, if he qualifies. I think true snooker fans will go to watch the Premier League if it''s near where they live whoever is playing. I think the support will still be high and a little bit of extra marketing has been done to help that along. I don''t think Ronnie''s decision not to play is anything to do with the actual way the Premier League is run. In fact, I know he enjoys playing and is disappointed not to be involved this season. He had his contractual issues and couldn''t sign up to play in the Premier League before that was sorted. He''s just so busy that something had to give. He''s not the kind of player who will enter everything and the Premier League is off the list this time, unfortunately. I know the event will still be a roaring success and that''s why it''s here to stay. The Premier League does put some constraints on when other tournaments can be played because so many top players are involved, but they all still want to play in this as much as a lot of ranking events. We''ve got ten top players this season and it''s going to be another exciting campaign. A lot of people are also looking forward to seeing Peter Ebdon under shot clock constraints. It will be interesting to see how he reacts. I haven''t spoke to him about it but he''s a very deep thinker and will definitely have it on his mind. I think 25 seconds is quite a long time to play a shot. I''d like to see it cut to 20 seconds. It would give all the players something to think about, for sure. It would get even people watching. People ask me whether the Premier League still has its place with so many other tournaments, but I still think there''s a big place for it. You only have to look at the reaction of the players when they qualify to see how popular it is. Stuart Bingham can''t wait for it, for example. There''s still a prestige to the event. I''m really looking forward to getting stuck into the Premier League again. It''s been a long time since the last one finished, but there have been so many tournaments that it''s really flown by. The Premier League is returning to more of a league format this time. There are no more Shoot Out frames. They are all six-frame matches again. I think it was difficult for people at home to follow last season and it took quite a lot of explanations before every match at the venue, so the idea is to simplify the event again. I wouldn''t say it was a failure last time but it might have been more successful if there was a programme at each event with a detailed explanation of the new format and I think the traditional Premier League fans didn''t enjoy the change. It was difficult for me as referee and all the players to suddenly have to switch formats during a match and handle two sets of rules in one match. It''s going to be great to go back to Grismby for the finals weekend. I think the auditorium deserves it. it''s been an excellent venue down the years and one of a handful where you can rely on a sell-out crowd. There are lots of people there who love the game and really know their snooker. You rarely have to ask for quiet. They''re great in every way. I''m not doing as much refereeing as much this season for World Snooker because I''ve got so heavily involved with the tournament office and organising events. I''ll always be doing some matches, of course. I had a quarter-final at the Wuxi Classic and a semi-final at the Australian Open to fill in for Jan Verhaas while he''s injured, but the Premier League is my main regular refereeing duty. There are only two dates I won''t be doing where Brendan Moore is stepping in for me. This is just because of how busy I am. I would never give the Premier League up because it''s my main priority. When I left World Snooker, Matchroom didn''t think twice about keeping me on. They said that as long as I wanted the job, it was mine. I''m giving the Premier League some loyalty into return for that. I hope you all enjoy this season''s Premier League as much as I know I will. Article courtesy of the OnCue Images Courtesy of: Kino Arcades & Action Images', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Lostwithiel pull away from the relegation zone', 'lostwithiel-pull-away-from-the-relegation-zone', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Lostwithiel ‘B’ gained two good points from Tywardreath ‘A’ giving them some daylight from Mevagissey ‘B’ and Sparks at the bottom.', null, '"Dave Morshead’s fine last-frame victory over Steve Bampton helped Bethel ‘A’ return to winning ways with a 3-2 success at Sparks."', 'Lostwithiel ‘B’ gained two good points from Tywardreath ‘A’ giving them some daylight from Mevagissey ‘B’ and Sparks at the bottom.

more Darren Kirwan’s 31 and Mike Hancock’s 48 helped the Legion boys keep in touch with Bugle ‘A’ as Andrew Best’s 36 and 35 breaks against Richard Broad helped the table toppers to a 3-2 win over Lostwithiel ‘A’. Dave Morshead’s fine last-frame victory over Steve Bampton helped Bethel ‘A’ return to winning ways with a 3-2 success at Sparks. St Neot may yet have a say in the title race after whitewashing Mevagissey ‘B’ 5-0. Aaron Court’s 41 followed by dad Steve’s 37 – the highlights of the evening. Bugle ‘B’ came away from Mevagissey ‘A’ with a 3-2 win as Taylor-Roscorla and D Rundle won their battles – Matt Fox and Mike Towsey the home winners. St Blazey cemented their place at the top of the Rees Memorial League trashing Bethel ‘B’ 4-1 whilst both Bethel ‘D’ and Tywardreath ‘B’ won 4-1 to enhance their promotion credentials. Mevagissey ‘D’ picked up a welcome win over Bugle ‘C’ as J Prynn, Scott Mills and Charlie Prynn triumphed.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Mevagissey ‘A’ 2 (M Fox, M Towsey) Bugle ‘B’ 3 (G Taylor, M Roscorla, D Rundle), Bugle ‘A’ 3 (A Best (36+35), I Powell, G Spencer) Lostwithiel ‘A’ 2 (D Lock, D Grose), Lostwithiel ‘B’ 2 (J Daniell, L Flower) Tywardreath ‘A’ 3 (D Kirwan (31), M Hancock (48), A Gooch), Sparks 2 9M Williams, R Bolt) Bethel ‘A’ 3 (B Stephens, G Stephens, D Morshead), St Neot 5 (M Courts, A Tamblyn, A Courts (41), M Rowe, S Courts (37)) Mevagissey ‘B’. Rees Memorial League: Mevagissey ‘D’ 3 (J Prynn, S Mills, C Prynn) Bugle ‘C’ 2 (N Couchman, G Stocks), Tywardreath ‘B’ 4 (G Kent, K Wilks, N Rowe, P Hunt) Bethel ‘C’ 1 (H Brett), Bethel ‘B’ 1 St Blazey 4, Bethel ‘D’ 4 (A Spearman, V Hathway, J Pascoe, T Stephen) Mevagissey ‘C’ 1 (N Shaw). Top breaks of the week: Darren Kirwan 31, Mike Hancock 38, Andrew Best 36 & 35, Aaron Court 41, Steve Court 37. Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Magical Maximum Seals Rocket Triumph', 'magical-maximum-seals-rocket-triumph', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'With a new magic moment in his amazing career, Ronnie O''Sullivan made a 147 to complete a 9-3 win over Ding Junhui in the BetVictor 伟德 Welsh Open final.', null, '"O''Sullivan said: "I wasn''t really going for it. I was trying to score as many points as I could to get over the line. It wasn''t until I got to 48 and the balls broke lovely that I thought it was on, but I was more concerned with winning the frame. I was still playing shots to win the frame until I got to 72, then I thought I can get the maximum. The fans deserve it - they have supported this tournament brilliantly over the years. In front of a crowd like this it is a great experience. It''s a nice record, to have 12 maximums. I suppose a little bit of me wanted to take one of Hendry''s records."', 'With a new magic moment in his amazing career, Ronnie O''Sullivan made a 147 to complete a 9-3 win over Ding Junhui in the BetVictor 伟德 Welsh Open final.

more O''Sullivan sealed victory in the perfect fashion, potting 15 reds with blacks and clearing the colours in the last frame. He even potted the last black left-handed to complete a demonstration of pure genius. It''s the 12th 147 of his career, beating the record of 11 he had held jointly with Stephen Hendry. The break was worth £12,000 to go with the £60,000 he collected for winning his first ranking title of the season. It''s the 105th maximum in snooker history and O''Sullivan''s first since 2011. O''Sullivan won the 26th ranking title of his career, moving him ahead of John Higgins on the all-time list and just two behind Steve Davis'' total of 28, though still ten behind Hendry''s record of 36. The 38-year-old from Chigwell''s did the bulk of the damage in the first session, surging 7-1 ahead as China''s Ding made a host of uncharacteristic errors. And O''Sullivan added the two frames he needed, bringing a 1,100-strong crowd at the Newport Centre to their feet with a grandstand finish. O''Sullivan has played in 12 tournaments this season and has now won four of those, having previously captured the Paul Hunter Classic, Champion of Champions and the Masters, all non-ranking events. It''s his third Welsh Open title and rarely can any player have won a tournament so convincingly; he conceded just ten frames in seven matches (winning 36) and never looked in danger of defeat. Playing the best snooker of his career in his late 30s, O''Sullivan has won the World Championship in each of the last two years and is sure to be favourite to make it a hat-trick at the Crucible in the Spring. Joe Perry hinted at the aura O''Sullivan commands when he tweeted: ''It''s hard to describe how different it is playing Ronnie as opposed to any other player.'' O''Sullivan is the first British winner of a ranking event this season, with the previous titles shared between Ding, Neil Robertson and Marco Fu. Once again, a tournament that started with 128 players in the first round finished with two of the best in the final. O''Sullivan and Ding - alongside Robertson - have been the best in the world this season. Having won four ranking titles this season, Ding missed out on the chance to equal Hendry''s record of five, set in 1990/91. The Sheffield-based cueman has been remarkably consistent in recent months and had won 39 out of 41 ranking event matches before today, but was unable to improve that record after getting off to a poor start. O''Sullivan made top breaks of 91, 70 and 92 in the first session as he opened up a six-frame advantage. Ding got off to the perfect start in the evening as back-to-back centuries, 109 and 121, raised his hopes of a fight-back. In frame 11, O''Sullivan led 56-0 when he ran out of position, and a loose safety gave Ding a chance. But he made just 13, and O''Sullivan got back in to add 49 for 8-3. And the contest was soon over thanks to the Rocket''s electric finish, as he compiled the 147 in seven minutes, 31 seconds. O''Sullivan said: "I wasn''t really going for it. I was trying to score as many points as I could to get over the line. It wasn''t until I got to 48 and the balls broke lovely that I thought it was on, but I was more concerned with winning the frame. I was still playing shots to win the frame until I got to 72, then I thought I can get the maximum. The fans deserve it - they have supported this tournament brilliantly over the years. In front of a crowd like this it is a great experience. It''s a nice record, to have 12 maximums. I suppose a little bit of me wanted to take one of Hendry''s records. "I played a really good match, he missed a few shots early on. I pounced in the first few frames and was scoring heavily. After that I lost my concentration a bit and was losing position, but I won a lot of the close frames which was important. Anyone that comes back at you, especially of Ding''s quality, you have to take it seriously. I have learnt how to cope with my emotions and knew I had to keep calm. I hadn''t done a lot wrong so I couldn''t be too hard on myself. "I am happy I have won and have had a good week. It is just about getting the CV looking as nice as you possibly can. The enjoyment is in the doing so I am glad the tournament is over now. I am trying to get the balance with enjoying home life and snooker." Ding said: "I know I wasn''t in my best form and had a lot of mistakes today. Ronnie punished me straight away when I missed easy shots and he was scoring very heavily. I never thought that the game was finished and kept trying. At 7-3 I had a chance but I didn''t take it. It was great to play Ronnie in the final. He can make a 147 any time he wants. I thought it was possible from the first black. I just like people enjoying the game. People love to watch Ronnie play - he is fast and attacking. "I have made five finals this season and I had never done that before so it has been very good. It doesn''t matter if I won or lost. I hope I can get more experience for the next tournament and keep winning to get back my form. I hope I can win more ranking titles." Article courtesy of the World Snooker Images Courtesy of: Tai Chengzhe', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Maguire Is Lisbon Lion', 'maguire-is-lisbon-lion', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Stephen Maguire won the new Lisbon Open in Portugal, beating Matthew Selt 4-2 in the final.', null, '"Apart from my first match and the final which were a bit scrappy, I played well over the two days," said 33-year-old Maguire. "I won''t get carried away with this and it still hurts that I lost to Judd in the semis of the UK last week."', 'Stephen Maguire won the new Lisbon Open in Portugal, beating Matthew Selt 4-2 in the final.

more Only last month, after losing his opening match at the Champion of Champions event against Judd Trump, Glasgow''s Maguire admitted he was thinking of quitting snooker. Since then he has reached at least the semi-finals of three consecutive events, and he has now won a tournament which counts towards the world rankings for the first time since the 2013 Welsh Open. With victory over Selt - who was playing in his first professional final - world number 12 Maguire took the 25,000 Euro top prize at the season''s penultimate Kreativ Dental European Tour event. World number 42 Selt, who knocked out the likes of Judd Trump and Barry Hawkins, took the opening frame of the final with a break of 78. Maguire nicked the next by clearing from brown to black, then compiled a run of 100 to lead 2-1. Selt levelled with a break of 69 and led 30-0 in frame five. But Maguire took it with runs of 50 and 26, then made an 86 in the next to seal victory. "Apart from my first match and the final which were a bit scrappy, I played well over the two days," said 33-year-old Maguire. "I won''t get carried away with this and it still hurts that I lost to Judd in the semis of the UK last week. But it feels good to lift a trophy again and to be the last man standing. I''ll have a break over Christmas and hopefully win more next year." The first ever professional tournament in Portugal was enthusiastically received by the local community, with fans flocking to the Casal Vistoso Sports Centre in Lisbon to watch many of the world''s best players. Maguire added: "I didn''t expect to see big crowds here, but the fans were great - they made the tournament." The other highlight of the tournament was a 147 from Ben Woollaston - the first maximum of his career - on Friday. The 2014/15 Kreativ Dental European Tour series comprises six events across the continent, with the top 24 in the final Order or Merit to go through to the Players Championship next March to battle for the £100,000 jackpot. The next and final event is the Gdynia Open in Poland from February 26 to March 1. Results (last 16 onwards) Last 16 Matthew Selt 4-1 Dominic Dale Judd Trump 4-2 Aditya Mehta Joe Perry 4-3 Jamie Jones Barry Hawkins 4-3 Peter Ebdon Stephen Maguire 4-2 John Higgins Craig Steadman 4-0 Stuart Bingham Mark Davis 4-1 Joel Walker Noppon Saengkham 4-3 Kurt Maflin Quarter-finals Selt 4-1 Trump Hawkins 4-2 Perry Maguire 4-2 Saengkham Davis 4-3 Steadman Semi-finals Selt 4-2 Hawkins Maguire 4-0 Davis Final Maguire 4-2 Selt Article courtesy of the World Snooker Images Courtesy of: Monique Limbos', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Maguire Is Six Red King', 'maguire-is-six-red-king', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Stephen Maguire edged out Ricky Walden 8-7 in the final to win the SangSom Six Red World Championship in Bangkok.', null, null, 'Stephen Maguire edged out Ricky Walden 8-7 in the final to win the SangSom Six Red World Championship in Bangkok.

more In an exciting final, Walden, who won this title in 2008, looked on control when he led 6-3, but Glasgow''s Maguire kept his hopes alive by winning the next two frames. Chester''s Walden moved to the brink of victory at 7-5, only for world number 13 Maguire to take the last three frames to win the trophy. It''s 33-year-old Maguire''s first title since the 2013 Welsh Open and he banks 2.5 million Thai Baht (around £48,000), while Walden won 1 million Baht. Both players now fly to China for the Shanghai Masters world ranking event, which starts on Monday. Results: Final Ricky Walden 7-8 Stephen Maguire Semi-finals Ricky Walden 7-1 Dominic Dale Kritsanut Lertsattayathorn 2-7 Stephen Maguire Quarter-finals Ricky Walden 7-6 Mark Williams Dominic Dale 7-4 Hossein Vafaei Ayouri Kritsanut Lertsattayathorn 7-4 John Higgins Stephen Maguire 7-4 Shaun Murphy Last 16 Dominic Dale 6-3 Dechawat Poomjaeng Ehsan Hydarinezhad 3-6 Hossein Vafaei Ayouri Mark Williams 6-3 Joe Perry Shachar Ruberg 2-6 Ricky Walden Stephen Maguire 6-0 Jamie Rhys Clarke Noppon Saengkham 3-6 Shaun Murphy Kritsanut Lertsattayathorn 6-2 Mark Davis John Higgins 6-5 Stuart Bingham Last 32 Matthew Stevens 3-6 Noppon Saengkham Stephen Maguire 6-4 Ben Judge Jamie Rhys Clarke 6-5 Ken Doherty James Wattana 4-6 Stuart Bingham Michael White 4-6 Shaun Murphy Ryan Day 4-6 Kritsanut Lertsattayathorn Mark Davis 6-1 Michael Holt John Higgins 6-3 Kamal Chawla Dechawat Poomjaeng 6-5 Liang Wenbo Dominic Dale 6-2 Robert Milkins Thepchaiya Un-Nooh 5-6 Ehsan Hydarinezhad Joe Perry 6-2 Boonyarit Kiattikul Mark Williams 6-0 Kacper Filipiak Muhammad Asif 4-6 Hossein Vafaei Ayouri Graeme Dott 5-6 Shachar Ruberg Barry Hawkins 2-6 Ricky Walden Article courtesy of the World Snooker', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Maguire Prince Of Wales', 'maguire-prince-of-wales', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Stephen Maguire beat Stuart Bingham 9-8 in a dramatic BetVictor Welsh Open final to end a drought of five years without a ranking title', null, '"I lost a couple of finals last season which were painful. I should have beaten Ronnie in Germany, then I played Ebdon in China and I was fighting for my life but the last frame just got away from me. So to win this one is sweet."', 'Stephen Maguire beat Stuart Bingham 9-8 in a dramatic BetVictor Welsh Open final to end a drought of five years without a ranking title

more A superb final which featured three centuries and 11 more breaks over 50 saw Maguire make a nerveless 82 in the deciding frame to capture the fifth world ranking title of his career and first since the 2008 China Open. A full house at the Newport Centre saw Glasgow''s 31-year-old Maguire receive the trophy and £50,000 at the end of a tremendous tournament featuring top class snooker, thrilling fight backs, clearances and tense finishes, excellent crowds and even this. Maguire punched the table as he crossed the winning line, revealing just how desperate he was to end a long spell without a major crown. He had lost his previous three ranking finals, each of which he could have won, and while he has taken Players Tour Championship titles over the past two seasons, it is success on the big stage that the gifted Scot craves. Bingham looked to be in control when he won five out of six frames to lead 7-5, with Maguire''s temperament showing signs of cracking, but the match hinged on a tense 13th frame which Maguire won on the last black. Ultimately it was the seventh Welsh Open final since 1999 to go to a deciding frame. After struggling for form in the first half of the season, Maguire sharpened up at the recent Championship League, though he had to come from 3-1 down to beat Mark Joyce 4-3 just to qualify for Newport. Now he''s up to number six in the world and is sure to be a danger in the latter part of the season. Bingham is enjoying the best season of his career, having won the Premier League and two APTC events. After 15 years struggling to make a breakthrough, he has made huge strides since winning the Australian Goldfields Open in 2011 and has proved his ability to produce his best on the big occasion. Despite just missing out on his second ranking title, he is up to a career high of number seven in the world. Bingham won the opening frame of the evening session with a break of 73 to take the lead for the first time at 5-4. In frame ten, Maguire missed a black off its spot at 37-16, but earned another chance with a clever plant on a red and made a 71 to restore parity. The big breaks continued to fly in as Bingham made his first century of the match, a 118, to lead 6-5. In frame 12, he made 36 before missing the blue when splitting the pack. Maguire lost his chance to counter after just one red when he missed a black off its spot. He got another chance but over-cut a red to a top corner and Bingham took advantage for 7-5 at the mid-session interval. A dramatic 13th frame came down to a safety battle on the final blue. Bingham laid a tight snooker behind the pink, but in doing so fluked the blue into a middle pocket. Almost touching the pink, he was unable to chip it into the same pocket. Maguire converted an excellent pot and added the black to close to within one frame. Bingham led 40-0 in the next when he missed a thin blue off its spot. He later failed on a long pot with four reds left, and Maguire knocked in a double to initiate an excellent 42 clearance to the blue which brought him level at 7-7. Early in the 15th, Bingham missed a tough red to a centre pocket, and Maguire''s clinical 77 saw him edge in front. The Scot looked set for victory in frame 16 until he missed a tricky red at 59-6. And when he failed to escape from a snooker with four reds left, Bingham fashioned a cool 58 clearance to force the decider. After some 15 minutes of jousting, Bingham decided to take on a risky plant on two reds, but missed his target. And gritty Maguire showed his quality with a fine 82 to clinch the tie. "I''m delighted, especially the way I finished," said Maguire. "I thought I had let it slip when it went 8-8 as Stuart made a great clearance and I wasn''t feeling good. I was chuffed to get a chance in the last frame and make it count. "I''ll feel great for the next couple of days and then it''s on to the next tournament. I want to push on and not have to wait another five years for the next one. There were times when I doubted whether I would ever do it again. It has hurt to see other players lift trophies, especially when I think I''m a better player than some of them." Reflecting on the final, Maguire added: "I though I was beat, I was almost resigned to losing, I was just trying to keep calm. It should have been 8-5, he refused some balls when he had the chance to kill me off. That spurred me on and I got the bit between my teeth. To win that frame to go 7-6 was massive. "I lost a couple of finals last season which were painful. I should have beaten Ronnie in Germany, then I played Ebdon in China and I was fighting for my life but the last frame just got away from me. So to win this one is sweet." Bingham said: "I feel as if I threw it away, I just tightened up a bit towards the end. It would have meant so much to win it so I''m gutted. I should have gone 8-5 up, I missed an easy pink, I was just scared of playing a push shot. Then in the next frame I was 40 ahead and missed a bad blue. I held myself together to make a clearance to go 8-8 but just couldn''t get a chance in the decider. I never go for plants unless they are 100 per cent, I thought the one I went for was perfect so maybe I got a kick. Stephen showed what a class act he is with his break in the last. "I thought I was the better player tonight, I took the match to him to go 7-5. But then I had chances and I blew it. It has been a great season and my game is in good shape, it just didn''t quite happen today." Article courtesy of the World Snooker Images Courtesy of: Monique Limbos', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Mark Selby v Mark Williams - Snooker Plus Exhibition', 'mark-selby-v-mark-williams-snooker-plus-exhibition', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Tickets now on sale for Mark Selby v Mark Williams - Saturday 10th November 2012', null, '"Come and enjoy an evening of snooker with the ''Jester'' from Leicester Mark Selby and two times World Champion Mark Williams"', 'Tickets now on sale for Mark Selby v Mark Williams - Saturday 10th November 2012

more Come and enjoy an evening of snooker with the ''Jester'' from Leicester Mark Selby and two times World Champion Mark Williams. The clash of the giants will take place at the Keay Theatre, St Austell, Cornwall. Both Marks will play 11 frames of snooker against each other with a 12th frame being auctioned to the highest bidder in the audience. The lucky winner will have the opportunity to choose which Mark to play. At the end of the evening everyone will have the chance to meet the players for photo''s and autographs

EXCITING EVENING TO BE HAD

Both Marks will play 11 frames of snooker against each other with a 12th frame being auctioned to the highest bidder in the audience. The lucky winner will have the opportunity to choose which Mark to play. At the end of the evening everyone will have the chance to meet the players for photo''s and autographs TICKETS: £27 DOORS OPEN:6.30pm START TIME: 7.30pm To purchase tickets contact the Cornish Riviera Box Office on 01726 879500. Box office opens from 9.00am to 5.00pm, Monday to Saturday. Images Courtesy of: Action Images', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Mark of a champion', 'mark-of-a-champion', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'His impressive 4-1 victory against world number one Mark Selby in the final confirms a lot of what we already know about Allen.', null, '"The Northern Ireland potter loves playing in front of a big crowd and always wants to put on a show. He sure did that in Belgium. Three consecutive centuries saw him race into a 3-0 lead and all but sealed his win against Selby; and a second ranking trophy."', 'His impressive 4-1 victory against world number one Mark Selby in the final confirms a lot of what we already know about Allen.

more The Northern Ireland potter loves playing in front of a big crowd and always wants to put on a show. He sure did that in Belgium. Three consecutive centuries saw him race into a 3-0 lead and all but sealed his win against Selby; and a second ranking trophy. Allen was honest enough in his interview with me to admit he finds motivation difficult to come by in the PTCs, perhaps explaining why until this weekend he was yet to win one. But an enthusiastic home Belgian crowd helped him to find that extra gear he often reserves for the very biggest tournaments. Allen played some super snooker this weekend and this is the reason everyone enjoys watching him play. This great performance shows he still enjoys competing and has a determination to find motivation in every tournament he plays. If he does go on to find greater consistency, it will be a sign of his growing maturity and will put him in a fantastic position to win the amount of titles his talent clearly deserves. Mark has fantastic confidence in his ability and, on this showing, it''s easy to see why. He may have underachieved in his career so far but, having reached three finals in the past year and winning two trophies in eight months, he''s firmly positioned himself alongside the game''s other leading lights. He deserves a great applause for the way he plays the game and I''m sure he will go on to win many more titles. Article courtesy of OnCue Image courtesy of Monique Limbos', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Marvellous McGill is Shoot Out King', 'marvellous-mcgill-is-shoot-out-king', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Anthony McGill won his second ranking title of the season by beating Xiao Guodong in the final of the Coral Shoot Out.', null, '“In the final I played a good positional shot on the last red to get on the black, and then the balls were all there so it was a matter of not collapsing. I was just hoping going into this season that I would win one tournament so two is brilliant.', 'Anthony McGill won his second ranking title of the season by beating Xiao Guodong in the final of the Coral Shoot Out.

more Glasgow’s 26-year-old McGill once again underlined his talent as he followed up his maiden victory at July’s Indian Open by capturing another piece of major silverware. The Scot is the only player other than Mark Selby to win more than one ranking title this season. After taking a few years to establish himself having turned pro in 2010, McGill has come of age over the past eight months and made the transition from promising up-and-comer to multiple tournament winner. McGill won seven matches at the Watford Colosseum during an event which was opened up to all 128 tour players and given ranking status for the first time. His £32,000 pay day makes him likely to be among the top 16 when the seeding cut off for the World Championship falls after the China Open, which would mean an automatic place at the Crucible in April. He is officially ranked 20th in the world which means that in the seven-year history of the Shoot Out it has never been won a player inside the top 16. China’s Xiao is the only player to have reached the final of the Shoot Out twice but was again denied the trophy, just as he was by Michael White two years ago. An early foul from McGill gave world number 58 Xiao the first chance of the final, but he could only make 15. A safety exchange ended when Xiao left a red over a top corner pocket, and McGill stayed cool to make an excellent break of 67 which gave him the title. “I’ve never won more than one match in this event before so I can’t work out how I have won it,” said McGill. “I had a bit of luck and played better the longer the event went on. By the end I felt really confident. “You have to enjoy this type of event and have fun. If something goes wrong it’s not the end of the world. But it is a ranking event so in the back of my mind I knew it was a big deal. “In the final I played a good positional shot on the last red to get on the black, and then the balls were all there so it was a matter of not collapsing. I was just hoping going into this season that I would win one tournament so two is brilliant. “I have been practising really hard since Christmas. After the Scottish Open in December I was fed up with the game. I played nine tournaments in 11 weeks, which is fantastic, but when errors creep into your game there is no time to practise. “I felt I needed two or three weeks on the practice table. Over Christmas I got my head down and worked hard because I knew February and March would be busy. Now it has paid off. Hopefully I’ll be in the top 16 for the Crucible now. “The table played beautifully all week and the crowds supported the tournament really well.” Earlier in the semi-finals, Xiao beat amateur Andy Hicks, who had reached the last four of a ranking event for the sixth time in his career and first time since 1996. McGill edged out Shaun Murphy on the colours after Murphy crucially missed the final green to a centre pocket when trailing by five points. Article & Images courtesy of the World Snooker', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Marvellous Murphy Takes Haikou Title', 'marvellous-murphy-takes-haikou-title', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Despite a late rally from Selby, who came from 7-2 down to 8-6, it was Murphy who captured the trophy and £85,000 top prize on the island of Hainan in southern China.', null, '"Today was a good match, we both played well in parts. I''m so happy to win a ranking event again, it has been a long time. Snooker is a very tough game now so these events are hard to win. There are three more tournaments to go this season and maybe there''s something left in the tank."', 'Despite a late rally from Selby, who came from 7-2 down to 8-6, it was Murphy who captured the trophy and £85,000 top prize on the island of Hainan in southern China.

more The feeling of holding a ranking title has been a long time coming for 31-year-old Murphy, whose last such victory was at the 2011 Players Tour Championship Grand Finals. He now has five ranking titles to his name in all, having won the 2005 World Championship, 2007 Malta Cup and 2008 UK Championship. After winning the invitational Brazil Masters in September 2011, Manchester-based Murphy went 29 months without winning a tournament of any sort. Determined to end the drought, he worked long and hard on all aspects of his game, and also embarked upon a strict health regime which saw him lose over three stone. Improvements in his game, his fitness and his self esteem have been reflected in impressive results since the New Year and he tasted success at the Gdynia Open European Tour event last month. This week''s triumph in the Far East is even more significant for Murphy, especially as it has come with victories over the likes of Ding Junhui, defending champion Mark Allen and close friend Selby. It''s a major step for the world number nine as he seeks to re-establish himself at snooker''s top table. Murphy dominated the early stages of the final, going 4-0 up with top breaks of 64, 80 and 52. World number two Selby finally got going by taking the fifth frame, then made a 91 to close to 4-2. But Murphy, blessed with a laser-straight cue action, found a rich seam of break-building with 98, 105 and 112 to establish a 7-2 advantage at the end of the first session. Leicester''s Selby, who was seeking his fourth ranking title, started the evening session strongly with a break of 78 then cleared from the last red in the next to close to 7-4. As mistakes crept into Murphy''s game, Selby built momentum with runs of 43 and 34 to narrow the gap further. Murphy crucially won the last frame before the interval with a run of 60 for 8-5. Selby took the next and looked set to make it 8-7 until he broke down on 49, allowing Murphy to make a superb 78 clearance. In frame 16, Murphy built a 71-0 lead in three scoring visits, and although Selby clawed his way back and battled hard for snookers, Murphy finally potted the pink to ensure victory. "Mark was under no pressure at 7-2," said Murphy. "I knew he would come back and that I would have to wait for my chances. We''re very good friends and we''ve beaten each other many times. "Today was a good match, we both played well in parts. I''m so happy to win a ranking event again, it has been a long time. Snooker is a very tough game now so these events are hard to win. There are three more tournaments to go this season and maybe there''s something left in the tank. "There''s no secret to it, I''ve just been working very hard and practising every day for a long time. It worked in Poland and it''s worked here. On Tuesday I''ll be back on the practice table." Selby, who was also runner-up at the UK Championship and the Masters, said: "In the first session I started badly. Giving someone of Shaun''s capability a 7-2 lead is hard to come back from. Shaun played very well all day. He punished me and won some really important frames, especially the one to go 9-6." Article courtesy of the World Snooker Images Courtesy of: Tai Chengzhe', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Mehta 147', 'mehta-147', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Aditya Mehta became the first Indian player to make an official maximum break at the Arcaden Paul Hunter Classic in Germany on Friday.', null, '"It''s an amazing feeling," said Mehta. "The balls opened up and I knew it was an opportunity by the time I was on 32. I''ve only made one 147 in practice and one in a match in India."', 'Aditya Mehta became the first Indian player to make an official maximum break at the Arcaden Paul Hunter Classic in Germany on Friday.

more The world number 49 from Mumbai made his perfect break in the third frame of his last 32 match against Stephen Maguire at the European Tour event, trailing 2-0 at the time. It''s the first 147 since Ronnie O''Sullivan''s at the Welsh Open in March, and the 106th in snooker history. Mehta, who was runner-up to Ding Junhui at the inaugural Indian Open last season, will win £1,500 from the rolling 147 prize pot for European Tour events, as long as the break is not equalled. The break was refereed by local German official Luise Kraatz, who was working at a professional event for the first time and taking charge of only her third match. "It''s an amazing feeling," said Mehta. "The balls opened up and I knew it was an opportunity by the time I was on 32. I''ve only made one 147 in practice and one in a match in India. I hoped one day I would be lucky enough to make one in a pro event and today it happened out of the blue." Glasgow''s Maguire eventually won the match 4-2. Defending champion Ronnie O''Sullivan, playing his first tournament of the season, strolled into the last 16, conceding just one frame in three matches. He beat Noppon Saengkham 4-0, Robbie Williams 4-0 and Barry Hawkins 4-1. The Rocket''s top breaks of the day were 114, 105 and 101. Judd Trump was hauled back from 3-0 to 3-3 by amateur Mitchell Travis in the last 32 but recovered to win the decider with a break of 108. Xiao Guodong edged out Jamie Cope 4-3 with a run of 77 in the decider, while Rob Milkins made 77 and 55 in the last two frames to beat Michael White 4-3. Alan McManus saw off Scott Donaldson 4-1 while Mark Allen beat Luca Brecel 4-2 with a top break of 81. China''s Tian Pengfei made 120 and 70 in the last two frames to beat Mike Dunn 4-3. Article courtesy of the World Snooker Images Courtesy of: Monique Limbos', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Memories - By Leighton Varcoe + Submit your memories to us online!', 'memories-by-leighton-varcoe-submit-your-memories-to-us-online', 'Snooker Memories', '[]'::jsonb, 'Leighton Varcoe of Mevagissey Social Club shares his Snooker memories from personal experiences and from his extensive time in the St Blazey & District Snooker League.', null, null, 'Leighton Varcoe of Mevagissey Social Club shares his Snooker memories from personal experiences and from his extensive time in the St Blazey & District Snooker League.

more Over the coming coming months Leighton has kindly agreed to write a column for the website on his personal and extensive league memories, some if his stories are just so fascinating they just had to be caught on paper (and this website). We also have an interview coming up with Leighton and Jonathan Barron in which we hope to get some very exclusive content including i''m sure some hidden media gems long lost in the vaults of Mevagissey Social Club, but for now please read the first article below, entitled ''From old to new'' You can also read Leighton''s extensive CUEVIEW here, and if you have any memories long hidden from view that you would like to share please fill them in on the online form below.

Leighton''s Memories - Article One ''Now & Then'' click here to read more

Leighton''s Memories - Article Two ''Now & Then'' click here to read more

Leighton''s Memories - Article Three ''Now & Then'' click here to read more

Leighton''s Memories - Article Four ''Now & Then'' click here to read more', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Memories - Now & Then - By Leighton Varcoe', 'memories-now-then-by-leighton-varcoe', 'Snooker Memories', '[]'::jsonb, 'Mevagissey Club had a very strong team before the arrival of my age group (mid 1950''s), in those days the league teams consisted of 6 players per side, also in those days a 40+ break would be high enough to win the…', null, null, 'Mevagissey Club had a very strong team before the arrival of my age group (mid 1950''s), in those days the league teams consisted of 6 players per side, also in those days a 40+ break would be high enough to win the seasons highest break trophy.

more In those days most players were very good billiards players and the type of game (snooker) they played was to pot the easy balls on or play very good safety to get in again (a very difficult game to play against if played really properly). Teams to look out for in those days were Mount Charles, Mevagissey Club, Tregonissey WMC, and St Blazey Institute to name but a few. Since that era each different age group has raised the standard of more attacking play which has lead to bigger breaks being made, also the standard of the tables in the league have improved immensely e.g. more heating in clubs, under table heating, lighter balls etc, which all in all has made players more consistent. Any young promising player if he learnt to play billiards very well would improve his cue fall control and knowledge of angles when break building and working out safety shots, getting out of a snooker better. One thing missing nowadays is supporters watching the matches which helps the players raise their game anyone who''s being used to playing to packed out audiences find it hard to play with no-one watching.

MEMORIES - NOW & THEN - BY LEIGHTON VARCOE - ARTICLE TWO

The Meva Team from the 1950 to 1975 were the team to beat (as records prove) winning the St Blazey & District Snooker League a record number of times and numerous Cornwall titles. (Individual/Pairs/Team 5aside/Team 3 pairs aside a record number of times).

The Meva Team from the 1950 to 1975 were the team to beat (as records prove) winning the St Blazey & District Snooker League a record number of times and numerous Cornwall titles (Individual/Pairs/Team 5aside/Team 3 pairs aside a record number of times). In the mid 1950''s to late 1950''s we had 3 players who won the cornish youth under 21 championship namely Jonathan Barron/Clifford Rowe & Leghton Varcoe. Jonathan won Cornish Individual Championship & Youth Championship in the same season, but also we had most of our talented young players called up to do their national service which definitely hindered their advancement in the snooker world by 2 years. In 195 Jonathan retired from seniors match play snooker but continued playingg for his beloved mega social club up until 1999/2000 season. After this mega pulled out of the league. In the period 1975-2000 (25 years) Jonathan never practised again but made the leagues 1st ever 100 break (103) at the Looe Social Club. Meva snooker for several seasons was non existent and then a few seasons ago a revival started. Firstly the A Team next season the B Team then C Team (this season) although the standard not so high as in the glory years. But we are on our way. Besides team players several other members enjoy their snooker. During the winter (Nov to Easter time) Meva social club runs a snooker school on Wednesday evenings from 7pm - 10pm Starting this winter on Wednesday November 14th 2012. The object of the snooker school is to improve your game (male & female) so you can enjoy playing snooker better, anyone can come along but would have to be a member of the mega social club. We are also hoping through snooker school to unlock some snooker talent then these types of players will be invited to join one of our 4 teams squads of players. League teams when they play at Meva Social are looked after quite well and enjoy refreshments for their teams during the evening. In Meva''s glory years this team spirit was captured with all our players being born and living within a radius of 1 mile from the club, this created a real family atmosphere amongst all the players and teams, this is what we hope to continue to bring to St Blazey & District League snooker and indeed snooker in general at Meva social club.

MEMORIES - NOW & THEN - BY LEIGHTON VARCOE - ARTICLE THREE

First of all I would like to congratulate the Tywardreath Royal British Legion Club ''A'' Team on how well they all played on Tuesday 6th November 2012 v Meva Social Club ''A''Team in the Victory snooker league at Tywardreath.

The Meva team were virtually spectators all night and although suffering a very heavy defeat enjoyed the quality of snooker played y the Tywardreath snooker boys - well done. In by gone days Victory league consisted of 20 teams which meant 38 league matches each season for the teams involved, a lot longer season than nowadays stretching from the end of August to the end of April. For any player loosing only 2 or 3 frames per season was excellent going when there was League Championship, Cornish Championship, National Championship players playing in the league as well. If you played for Cornwall there were lots of inter county matches as well, how we used to fit them all in I will never know, but we did and did exceptionally well in them. To me these were the best days of amateur snooker in Cornwall. Nowadays or since snooker went open there is not so much for the non pro player to enter, but these players do have the consolation to turn pro and try their skills on the pro circuit. But to get within reach of the top of pro snooker you have to be really talented with a superb temperament when in the match arena, but at least anyone has a chance to have a go.

MEMORIES - NOW & THEN - BY LEIGHTON VARCOE - ARTICLE FOUR

In the past each club were very well supported but nowadays most clubs play their matches with very little support but back in the Meva hey day when Meva played at home our snooker rooms were packed with enthusiastic supporters which created a great atmosphere to perform in.

When playing away or at a neutral venue most clubs whether they were large or small rooms had packed rooms (the main reason being people come along to see Jonathan Barron in action) so in time all our players got used to playing in front of packed audiences, with that in mind we found that if we played at venues with very small audiences the atmosphere wasn''t there which made it more difficult to perform (no places for faint hearts). Horace Fugler of Tregonissey WMC was a former County Champion from many years ago once stated that most teams coming to play Mevagissey at Meva lost the match mentally en route to Meva for their match. Nowadays St Blazey & District Snooker League is still running with 2 leagues - namely the Victory League & the Rees Memorial League with only 11 clubs providing the 21 teams that make up the 2 leagues. Listed below are clubs that used to be in the leagues which was reformed at the end of the 2nd World War. The league is also looking for new teams to boost our 2 leagues, so if any club, holiday park, hotel, pub teams who used to be in the league or any venue that has very good playing conditions would like to join the leagues please get in touch. Gorran St Austell RBL St Austell Con Club Services Club Grants Snooker Club (now a restaurant) St Dennis WMC Penwithick Luxulyan Biscovey St Blazey Railway Club Holmbush Institute (now a beauty parlour) Mount Charles Social Club St Blazey Institute (now no more) Trevelmond Pelynt (league champions 1962) Polperro Rowett Institute West Looe Reading Room (now a social club) Bodmin Con Club St Mabyn (Bodmin Moor) Mill Pool Lostwithiel Con Club Fowey RBL Carnoggas Bowling Club (Polgooth) Who let Tregonissey WMC use their snooker facilities while they had their new snooker room built.', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Meva back to winning ways with win over Bugle', 'meva-back-to-winning-ways-with-win-over-bugle', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Mevagissey ‘A’ returned to form and gained a one point advantage Tregonissey ‘A’ with a 4-1 victory at home to Bugle ‘B’.', null, 'Damon Rowe knocked in a 32 and a 47 to help St Blazey ‘A’ secure a 3-2 home win over Bethel ‘C’. Vince Hathway and Leigh Rosevear also won with Martin Powell and Howard Brett replying.', 'Mevagissey ‘A’ returned to form and gained a one point advantage Tregonissey ‘A’ with a 4-1 victory at home to Bugle ‘B’.

more The visitors night started well with Mark Sturtridge seeing off Chris Kendall but Phil Staley steading the boar beating Keith Moore and further wins for Matt Mills, Pete Chambers and Paul Williams, who knocked in a 36, beat Dave Rundle got the leaders a deserved win. After going 3-0 up Tregonissey ‘A’ will be rueing dropping the last two frames to a plucky Pelynt side. Ryan Bilson, Jason Simmons, who narrowly beat Richard Broad, and Chris Stephenson put them in a great position but Andy Parnell’s victory over Martin Knight followed by Phil Ormand’s win over Jason Cocks leaves them three points behind Meva having played the same number of games. St Blazey ‘B’ leapfrogged Bugle ‘A’ into third spot thanks to a 4-1 victory at Bethel ‘A’. Frame of the evening was Mike Hancock’s win over Gary Stephens which was a credit to both players. Paul Baron, Graham Taylor and Andrew Gooch completed Blazey’s points with Geoff Hawken defeating a new slim-size Darren Kirwan, who made his first appearance of the season. Damon Rowe knocked in a 32 and a 47 to help St Blazey ‘A’ secure a 3-2 home win over Bethel ‘C’. Vince Hathway and Leigh Rosevear also won with Martin Powell and Howard Brett replying. Cyril Dickenson and Darren Lock both won to give St Neot a flying start at home to Bethel ‘D’ but Steve Benallack and Ryan Tonkin both knocked in 30 breaks against Anthony Maddams and Chris Perring to level things up but Steve Courts put a good 35 together against Ross McMenemy to seal the home win. In the Rees League Lerryn moved off the bottom of the table with a comfortable 4-1 home win over now bottom St Stephen ‘B’. Terry Middleton, Dave Vipond, Graham Hoskin and Chris McMiram claimed their points with Craig Nicholls continuing his good recent form beating John Hancock. In a re-arranged Mevagissey derby the ‘C’ team defeated their ‘D’ team 3-2. James Prynn saw off Neil Parker in the opener and the Predator’s good potting, with an 18 break thrown in, made him the star of the night inflicting only a second defeat of the season on Rich Lakeman. George Lory and Rob Blamey levelled things beating the Chesterfield brothers with Ben Prynn’s narrow victory over Ian Latham claiming the bragging rights. Bethel ‘B’ went 2-0 up at home to Bodmin Q-Bar through Dave Morshead and Guy Buckle but Steve Bampton and Rich Bolt levelled things up only for Clive Stuthridge to hammer Brian Barnicoat leaving the second promotion spot wide open. There will be no catching leaders Mevagissey ‘B’ as they kept up their unbeaten record after seeing off St Stephen ‘A’. Dan Allen and Anthony Harrison played well when winning their frames but victories for Mark Gregory, Matt Rowe and Ben Rothwell saw the leaders home. Mike Towsey made his debut for Gorran Haven and had a winning start against Tregonissey ‘B’. Richard Holmes also continued his good season with a victory but wins for Phil and Justin Hooley left Gary Wherry to win the decider for the Treggy boys.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: St Blazey ‘A’ 3 (Vince Hathway, Damion Rowe (32+47), Leigh Rosevear) Bethel ‘C’ 2 (Martin Powell, Howard Brett), Tregonissey ‘A’ 3 (Ryan Bilson, Jason Simmons, Chris Stephenson) Pelynt 2 (Andy Parnell, Phil Ormond), Mevagissey ‘A’ 4 (Phil Staley, Matt Mills, Pete Chambers, Paul Williams (36)) Bugle ‘B’ 1 (Mark Sturtridge), Bethel ‘A’ 1 (Geoff Hawken) St Blazey ‘B’ 4 (Mike Hancock, Paul Baron, Andrew Gooch, Graham Taylor), St Neot 3 (Cyril Dickenson, Darren Lock, Steve Courts (35)) Bethel ‘D’ 2 (Steve Benallack (30), Ryan Tonkin (30)). Rees Memorial League: St Stephen ‘A’ 2 (Dan Allen, Anthony Harrison) Mevagissey ‘B’ 3 (Mark Gregory, Matt Rowe, Ben Rothwell), Bethel ‘B’ 3 (Dave Morshead, Guy Buckle, Clive Stuthridge) Bodmin Q-Bar 2 (Steve Bampton, Rich Bolt), Lerryn 4 (Terry Middleton, Dave Vipond, Graham Hoskin, Charles McMiram) St Stephen ‘B’ 1 (Craig Nicholls), Gorran Haven 2 (Mike Towsey, Richard Holmes) Tregonissey ‘B’ 3 (Philip Hooley, Justin Hooley, Gary Wherry), Mevagissey ‘C’ 3 (James Prynn, Dave Tregale, Ben Prynn) Mevagissey ‘D’ 2 (George Lory, Rob Blamey). Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Top breaks of the week: D Rowe 32+47, P Williams 36, S Courts 35, S Benallack 30, R Tonkin 30 Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Meva close the gap at the top', 'meva-close-the-gap-at-the-top', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'In the first week of the play-offs second-placed Mevagissey ‘A’ beat leaders Tregonissey ‘A’ 3-2 to close the gap at the top to six points.', null, '"Bethel ‘C’ drew level on points with Mevagissey ‘B’ by virtue of a 3-2 home win. Roger Smithson, Martin Powell who knocked in a 30 and Mike Williams got their points with Mark Gregory and Dave Kendall replying."', 'In the first week of the play-offs second-placed Mevagissey ‘A’ beat leaders Tregonissey ‘A’ 3-2 to close the gap at the top to six points.

more Paul Williams saw off Martin Knight with the help of a 45 and a 33 break. Wins by Chris Stephenson and Ryan Bilson put Treggy 2-1 up but Chris Kendall and Pete Chambers, with the help of two 33 breaks, saw off Barrie McIntosh and Meva home. St Blazey ‘B’ pipped Lostwithiel ‘A’ 3-2 with five breaks of over 30 on the night. Mike Hancock with a 43 and Paul Baron with a 39 and a 37 clearance won their games with Darren Kirwan’s 32 and Steve Courts’ 30 all in vain as they lost to Darren Lock and Paul Baron respectively. Bugle ‘B’ continued their excellent form beating St Blazey ‘A’ 4-1. Jake Moore (31) and Ian Ball (47) followed by wins for Dave Rundle and Keith Moore gave Bugle the win with Nick Kendall replying. Bethel ‘C’ drew level on points with Mevagissey ‘B’ by virtue of a 3-2 home win. Roger Smithson, Martin Powell who knocked in a 30 and Mike Williams got their points with Mark Gregory and Dave Kendall replying. In the Rees League top half Bethel ‘B’ trounced Tregonissey ‘A’ 5-0 to close to within two points of the runners-up spot. Four very close frames on the night all went the way of the home side as they put the pressure on their own ‘D’ team, who were beaten 3-2 at St Stephen ‘A’. Steve Benallack and Ryan Tonkin were on target for the ‘D’ team but Anthony Harrison, Dan Allen and John Bennett responded. Lerryn picked up another victory at home to St Stephen ‘B’. Rob Garside and Craig Nicholls took St Stephen’s points but wins for Terry Middleton, Graham Hoskin and Dave Phipps secured the home win. Mevagissey ‘D’ scored a good 4-1 home win over Gorran Haven. Rob Bunney took the Haven’s point beating Neil Parker but Mills, Tomlinson, Blamey and Lakeman saw Meva to the victory.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Mevagissey ‘A’ 3 (P Williams (45+33), C Kendall, P Chambers (2x33)) Tregonissey ‘A’ 2 (C Stephenson, R Bilson), St Blazey ‘A’ 3 (M Hancock (43), P Baron (39 – 37 clearance), A Gooch) Lostwithiel ‘A’ 2 (C Perring, D Lock), Bethel ‘C’ 3 (R Smithson, M Powell (30), M Williams) Mevagissey ‘B’ 2 (M Gregory, D Kendall), Bugle ‘B’ 4 (D Rundle, J Moore (31), K Moore, I Ball (47)) St Blazey ‘A’ 1 (N Kendall). Rees Memorial League: Bethel ‘B’ 5 (J Tamblyn, C Stuthridge, D Morshead, G Buckle, R Smithson) Tregonissey ‘B’ 0, St Stephen ‘A’ 3 (A Harrison, D Allen, J Bennett) Bethel ‘D’ 2 (S Benallack, R Tonkin), Lerryn 3 (T Middleton, G Hoskin, D Phipps) St Stephen ‘B’ 2 (R Garside, C Nicholls), Mevagissey ‘D’ 4 (S Mills, J Tomlinson, R Blamey, R Lakeman) Gorran Haven 1 (R Bunney). Top breaks of the week: P Williams 45+33, P Chambers 2x33 M Hancock 43, P Baron 39+37, I Ball 47, J Moore 31, M Powell 30 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Meva continue to rule the roost', 'meva-continue-to-rule-the-roost', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Top game of the Victory League saw leaders Mevagissey ‘A’ beat second-placed Bugle ‘A’ 3-2 to maintain a three-point lead at the top.', null, 'A topsy-turvy night at St Blazey ‘A’ saw Bethel ‘A’ sneak home 3-2. Chris Brown fought back excellently to beat Andy Peers but the frame of the evening was between Matt Green and Mickey Mears. High-class potting and a thrilling finish saw Matt prevail.', 'Top game of the Victory League saw leaders Mevagissey ‘A’ beat second-placed Bugle ‘A’ 3-2 to maintain a three-point lead at the top.

more In a match of high level snooker Pete Chambers put Meva into the lead but Ian Powell levelled things seeing off Matt Mills. In a pivotal third frame Gary Spencer was on a break but feathered the white ball when cueing for a black ball which allowed Paul Williams to put together the season’s top break of 71 (clearance) to regain the lead. Andrew Best’s 39 break against Leighton Varcoe levelled things only for Chris Kendall to record a final frame victory over Ian Ball. St Blazey ‘B’ won 5-0 at Bethel ‘C’ to move into second place. Paul Baron and Jamie Read both sneaked through on the black against fit-again Paul Thomas and Martin Powell with wins also for Graham Taylor, Mike Hancock and David Grose. Pelynt raced into a 3-0 lead over Bethel ‘D’ thanks to John Daniell, Andy Parnell and Richard Broad but were pegged back by Jason Pascoe (Len Flower) and Ryan Tonkin (Phil Orman) which lifts Bethel level on points with Bugle ‘B’ but having played a game more. Tregonissey ‘A’s improved form continued beating St Neot 4-1. They went 4-0 up thanks to Jason Cocks, Ryan Bilson, Martin Knight and Chris Stephenson, who had a 30 break when beating Cyril Dickenson. In the final frame Darren Lock had a 41 when beating Jason Simmonds for the away side’s only point. A topsy-turvy night at St Blazey ‘A’ saw Bethel ‘A’ sneak home 3-2. Chris Brown fought back excellently to beat Andy Peers but the frame of the evening was between Matt Green and Mickey Mears. High-class potting and a thrilling finish saw Matt prevail. Gary Stephens beat Nick Kendall in the last frame to secure the win for the Bethel boys. Mevagissey ‘B’ continue to impress with a 4-1 home win over basement boys St Stephen ‘B’, for whom Craig Nicholls played well to overcome Matt Rowe. Bethel ‘B’ jumped to fourth place with a whitewash of Gorran Haven. Paul Grimmett ended pointless against a ruthless Clive Stuthridge followed by wins for Guy Buckle, Jim Tamblyn, Rich Allen and Dave Morshead, who cleared all the colours to beat Lee McKinstry. Graham Hoskin was Lerryn’s only success as they went down 4-1 at St Stephen ‘A’ who remain in third spot with a game in hand over the two above with Bodmin Q-Bar remaining second after beating Tregonissey ‘B’ 4-1. The next round of the team handicap will now be played on December 1 with the team pairs set for December 15.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bugle ‘A’ 2 (Ian Powell, Andrew Best (39)) Mevagissey ‘A’ 3 (Pete Chambers, Paul Williams (71), Chris Kendall), St Blazey ‘A’ 2 (Chris Brown, Leigh Rosevear) Bethel ‘A’ 3 (Matt Green, Geoff Hawken, Gary Stephens), Tregonissey ‘A’ 4 (Jason Cocks, Ryan Bilson, Martin Knight, Chris Stephenson (40)) St Neot 1 (Darren Lock (41), Bethel ‘C’ 0 St Blazey ‘B’ 5 (Graham Taylor, Mike Hancock, David Grose, Paul Baron, Jamie Read), Pelynt 3 (John Daniell, Andy Parnell, Richard Broad) Bethel ‘D’ 2 (Jason Pascoe, Ryan Tonkin). Rees Memorial League: St Stephen ‘A’ 4 (Dan Allen, John Bennett, Cary Clarke, Anthony Harrison) Lerryn 1 (Graham Hoskin), Bethel ‘B’ 5 (Clive Stuthridge, Guy Buckle, Jim Tamblyn, Dave Morshead, Rich Allen) Gorran Haven 0, Mevagissey ‘B’4 (Mark Gregory, Dave Kendall, Ben Rothwell Nathan Shaw) St Stephen ‘B’ 1 (Craig Nicholls), Bodmin Q-Bar 4 Tregonissey ‘B’ 1. Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Top breaks of the week: P Williams 71, A Best 39, C Stephenson 40, D Lock 41 Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Meva leapfrog St Blazey and Bugle to top table', 'meva-leapfrog-st-blazey-and-bugle-to-top-table', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'With St Blazey ‘B’ and Bugle ‘A’ not playing Mevagissey ‘A’ jumped to the top of the Victory League after collecting all five frames at Bugle ‘B’.', null, 'While Pelynt picked up two handy points at home to Tregonissey. Richard Broad hammered Jason Cocks in the first game but wins for Barrie McIntosh, Ryan Bilson, who had a 33 clearance, and Chris Stephenson put Treggy 3-1 up only for Len Flower to pip Martin Knight in the last frame of the night.', 'With St Blazey ‘B’ and Bugle ‘A’ not playing Mevagissey ‘A’ jumped to the top of the Victory League after collecting all five frames at Bugle ‘B’.

more Four frames went down to the black but all went the way of the Meva boys to put them three points clear. St Neot moved up to fourth place after seeing off Bethel ‘D’. Jason Pascoe played excellently to beat Mark Pearce but Chris Perrings’ first win of the season followed by wins for Darren Lock, 35 break, Anthony Maddams and Steve Courts saw St Neot home. Bethel ‘C’ picked up their first win of the season by beating St Blazey ‘A’ 3-2. Leigh Rosevear and a majestic performance from Mickey Meares put the away side 2-1 up but Roger Smithson and Martin Powell followed up Howard Brett’s excellent win over Nick Kendall to see the basement boys home. While Pelynt picked up two handy points at home to Tregonissey. Richard Broad hammered Jason Cocks in the first game but wins for Barrie McIntosh, Ryan Bilson, who had a 33 clearance, and Chris Stephenson put Treggy 3-1 up only for Len Flower to pip Martin Knight in the last frame of the night. Mevagissey ‘B’ picked up a fine 4-1 home win over St Stephen ‘A’ to move joint top of the Rees League. Only Dan Allen who remains unbeaten could stop the Meva boys as Nathan Shaw, Dave Kendall, Ben Rothwell and Mark Gregory bought home the bacon. Bodmin Q-Bar are level on points with the Meva boys after defeating Bethel ‘B’. Jim Tamblyn continued his good form beating Marcus. But wins for Jon Rosevear, John Green and Matty Woodward put the home side 3-1 up but Mike Williams pipped Malcom Meneer on the black for Bethel’s other point. Treggy ‘B’ won by the odd frame at home to Gorran Haven. Lee McKinstry sneaked past Sam Simon in the first and a blackball win for Paul Grimmett over Chris Robins put the Seasiders 2-1 up but Phil and Michael Hooley followed up Justin Hooley’s win over Richard Holmes to give the Treggy boys the points. Down at the wrong end of the table Lerryn picked up a 3-2 away win at St Stephen ‘B’. Rob Garside beat Charles McMiram in the first and Josh Doherty beat Graham Hoskin to put the youngsters 2-1 up. But Dave Phipps added to Gordon Brimicombe’s win to leave captain Terry Middleton beat Craig Nicholls on the black to secure Lerryn’s win.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bethel ‘C’ 3 (Howard Brett, Roger Smithson, Martin Powell) St Blazey ‘A’ 2 (Leigh Rosevear, Mickey Meares), Pelynt 2 (Richard Broad, Len Flower) Tregonissey ‘A’ 3 (Barrie McIntosh, Ryan Bilson, Chris Stephenson), Bugle ‘B’ 0 Mevagissey ‘A’ 5 (Chris Kendall, Paul Williams, Pete Chambers, Matt Mills, Phil Staley), Bethel ‘D’ 1 (Jason Pascoe) St Neot 4 (Chris Perring, Darren Lock (35), Anthony Maddams, Steve Courts). Rees Memorial League: Mevagissey ‘B’ 4 (Nathan Shaw, Dave Kendall, Ben Rothwell, Mark Gregory) St Stephen ‘A’ 1 (Dan Allen), Bodmin Q-Bar 3 (Jon Rosevear, John Green, Matt Woodward) Bethel ‘B’ 2 (Jim Tamblyn, Mike Williams), St Stephen ‘B’ 2 (Rob Garside, Josh Doherty) Lerryn 3 (Gordon Brimicombe, Dave Phipps, Terry Middleton), Tregonissey ‘B’ 3 (J Hooley, Phil Hooley, Michael Hooley) Gorran Haven 2 (Lee McKinstry, Paul Grimmett). Top breaks of the week: D Lock 35 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Meva lose the Runabout Shield to Bugle', 'meva-lose-the-runabout-shield-to-bugle', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'A week after claiming the Runabout Shield from Lostwithiel ‘A’ Mevagissey ‘B’ lost possession of it to Bugle ‘A’ after a 4-1 home defeat.', null, '"Mevagissey ‘A’ dropped to third place despite winning at Bugle ‘B’ 3-2. Chris Kendall’s fine 32 break coupled with wins for Pete Chambers and Paul Williams helped see them home with Dave Rundle and Jake Moore securing the final two frames for the home side. "', 'A week after claiming the Runabout Shield from Lostwithiel ‘A’ Mevagissey ‘B’ lost possession of it to Bugle ‘A’ after a 4-1 home defeat.

more They felt hard done by on the night as Bugle pinched two frames by flukes. Meva’s only point came from Dave Kendall after he won the battle of the posties, seeing off Andy Peers. Bethel ‘C’ were also disappointed not to pick up a couple of frames when losing 5-0 to St Blazey ‘B’. Roger Smithson played very well but lost out to Paul Baron and Martin Powell was inconsolable after missing a straight pink to win against Mike Hancock who had earlier produced a 38 break. St Blazey ‘A’ pipped Bethel ‘A’ by the odd frame as Mickey Meares, Stewart Dewings and Nick Kendall won with Geoff Hawken and Leon Collard, who fluked pink and black to win, on target for the visitors. Mevagissey ‘A’ dropped to third place despite winning at Bugle ‘B’ 3-2. Chris Kendall’s fine 32 break coupled with wins for Pete Chambers and Paul Williams helped see them home with Dave Rundle and Jake Moore securing the final two frames for the home side. Lostwithiel ‘B’ have jumped 10 points at the top of the Rees League thanks to their third 5-0 win on the trot, this time over Lerryn. Gordon Brimicombe pushed Richard Broad all the way but lost out on the black and the home side safely saw off the Lerryn challenge to give them a massive lead at present. Mevagissey ‘C’ are their closest rivals in second place after two victories this week. Their first came over Gorran Haven 5-1. Richard Holmes producing an excellent victory over Joe Chesterfield for the Haven’s point but the star of the night once again was Dave ‘The Predator’ Tregale who beat Lee McKinstry sealed a good night’s work for the ‘C’ team, who had another win by the odd frame over St Stephen ‘B’ in a rearranged fixture. Craig Nicholls and Rob Pearce picked up two valuable points for the basement boys, who had to be content with a single point the previous evening, going down to Bethel ‘B’. Jon Rosevear saw off Jim Tamblyn for their point. Mevagissey ‘D’ bounced back from last week’s drubbing to overcome Bugle ‘C’ 3-2 with Rob Blamey, Rich Lakeman and James Tomlinson their winners.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: St Blazey ‘A’ 3 (M Meares, N Kendall, S Dewings) Bethel ‘A’ 2 (G Hawken, L Collard), Bugle ‘B’ 2 (D Rundle, J Moore) Mevagissey ‘A’ 3 (P Chambers, C Kendall (32), P Williams), Bethel ‘C’ 0 St Blazey ‘B’ 5 (B Phillips, P Baron, D Kirwan, G Taylor, M Hancock (38), Mevagissey ‘B’ 1 (D Kendall) Bugle ‘A’ 4 (G Spencer, P Sturtridge, A Best, I Powell). Rees Memorial League: St Stephen ‘A’ 3 (D Allen, C Clarke, A Garside) Bethel ‘D’ 2 (R Tonkin, J Pascoe), Bugle ‘C’ 2 (K Moore, S Wingrave) Mevagissey ‘D’ 3 (R Blamey, J Tomlinson, R Lakeman), Lostwithiel ‘B’ 5 (R Broad, G Lory, L Flower, J Daniell, A Parnell) Lerryn 0, Bethel ‘B’ 4 (R Allen, D Morsheard, G Buckle, C Stuthridge) St Stephen ‘B’ 1 (J Rosevear), Mevagissey ‘C’ 4 (D Tregale, J Prynne, B Prynn, M Chesterfield) Gorran Haven 1 (R Holmes), Mevagissey ‘C’ 3 (J Prynn, D Tregale, M Chesterfield) St Stephen ‘B’ 2 (C Nicholls, R Pearce). Top breaks of the week: C Kendall 32, M Hancock 38 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Meva pull clear at the top', 'meva-pull-clear-at-the-top', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Mevagissey ‘A’ produced an excellent 4-1 home win over St Blazey ‘B’ to go three points clear at the top of the St Blazey and District Snooker Victory League.', null, 'Tregonissey ‘A’ moved up to equal third place with a 4-1 win over Bugle ‘B’. Jason Simmons squeezed passed Mark Sturtridge in the first but Keith Moore saw off Jason Cocks to level things but further wins for Ryan Bilson, Martin Knight and Chris Stephenson saw the Treggy boys home.', 'Mevagissey ‘A’ produced an excellent 4-1 home win over St Blazey ‘B’ to go three points clear at the top of the St Blazey and District Snooker Victory League.

more Captain Phil Staley started the ball rolling defeating Mike Hancock but Paul Baron levelled things seeing off Chris Kendall but wins for Pete Chambers, Paul Williams and veteran Leighton Varcoe, who recorded a 31 break to beat Jamie Read, saw the Meva boys clear at the summit. Bugle ‘A’ claimed all the points at home to luckless Bethel ‘C’. Mike Williams and Tony Green fought all the way in their frames but all the Bugle boys prevailed with Andrew Best notching a 31 break in his victory over Roger Smithson. Bethel ‘A’ moved up to fifth place after beating their ‘D’ side 4-1. Andy Peers and Gary Stephens put them two up and Matt Green posted a 32 break against Ryan Tonkin but only ended up with a 33 points total as Ryan Tonkin played some lovely snooker to win and it looked for the ‘D’ team with Andy Spearman well ahead against Leon Collard but wretched luck for Andy saw Leon sneak the frame on the black and Geoff Hawken beat Jason Pascoe in the last. St Blazey ‘A’ were 3-0 up against Pelynt through Nick Kendall, Chris Brown (38) and Stewart Dewings but Pelynt fought back to win the last two frames through John Daniell and Will Curtis to maintain their mid-table position. Tregonissey ‘A’ moved up to equal third place with a 4-1 win over Bugle ‘B’. Jason Simmons squeezed passed Mark Sturtridge in the first but Keith Moore saw off Jason Cocks to level things but further wins for Ryan Bilson, Martin Knight and Chris Stephenson saw the Treggy boys home. Mevagissey ‘B’ move four points clear in the Rees League and also captured the Runabout Shield after winning at Gorran Haven 4-1. Lee McKinstry inflicted a first defeat of the season for Dave Kendall but big victories for Ben Rothwell, Mark Gregory, Nathan Shaw and Matt Rowe put the Meva boys in a very strong position to make a quick return to the Victory League. Bethel ‘B’ took all five frames off St Stephen ‘B’. Rob Garside was very unlucky to lose to Rich Allen whilst Clive Stuthridge claimed the top break so far in the Rees with a 34 against Craig Nicholls with further wins for Dave Morshead, Jim Tamblyn and Guy Buckle. Mevagissey ‘D’ went 2-0 up at home to Tregonissey ‘B’ through Scott MIlls and Rich Lakeman but the Treggy boys hit back through Justin and Philip Hooley and Gary Wherry in a match which featured three black ball frames. St Stephen ‘A’ moved up to third place thanks to taking all five points at home to Mevagissey ‘C’. John Bennett recorded his first win of the season beating Charlie Prynn and there was also wins for Shaun Hicks, Anthony Garside, Cary Clark and the unbeaten Dan Allen.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bugle ‘A’ 5 (Paul Sturtridge, Ian Powell, Andrew Best (31), Gary Spencer, Ian Ball) Bethel ‘C’ 0, St Blazey ‘A’ 3 (Nick Kendall, Chris Brown (38), Stewart Dewings) Pelynt 2 (John Daniell, Will Cortis), Tregonissey ‘A’ 4 (Jason Simmons, Ryan Bilson, Martin Knight, Chris Stephenson) Bugle ‘B’ 1 (Keith Moore), Mevagissey ‘A’ 4 (Phil Staley, Pete Chambers, Paul Williams, Leighton Varcoe (31)) St Blazey ‘B’ 1 (Paul Baron), Bethel ‘A’ 4 (Andy Peers, Gary Stephens, Leon Collard, Geoff Hawken) Bethel ‘D’ 1 (Ryan Tonkin). Rees Memorial League: St Stephen ‘A’ 5 (Anthony Garside, Dan Allen, Shaun Hicks, John Bennett, Cary Clark) Mevagissey ‘C’ 0, Bethel ‘B’ 5 (Rich Allen, Dave Morshead, Jim Tamblyn, Guy Buckle, Clive Stuthridge (34) St Stephen ‘B’ 0, Mevagissey ‘D’ 2 (Scott Mills, Rich Lakeman) Tregonissey ‘B’ 3 (Justin Hooley, Philip Hooley, Gary Wherry), Gorran Haven 1 (Lee McKinstry) Mevagissey ‘B’ 4 (Ben Rothwell, Mark Gregory, Nathan Shaw, Matt Rowe). Top breaks of the week: A best 31, C Brown 38, L Varcoe 31, C Stuthridge 34 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Meva still lead the way in the Victory League', 'meva-still-lead-the-way-in-the-victory-league', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Mevagissey ‘A’ continue to impress at the top of the St Blazey and District Victory League after defeating Bethel ‘D’ 4-1 to stay three points clear of Bugle ‘A’.', null, 'St Stephen ‘A’ got a good 3-2 away win at Q-Bar to move up to third with a game in hand over the top two. Richie Bolt and Steve Bampton took the first two frames against Johnny Bennett and Shaun Hicks but excellent victories for Anthony Garside (Matt Woodward), Dan Allen (Marcus Causton) and Cary Clarke (Phil Ashton) saw the boys return home happy.', 'Mevagissey ‘A’ continue to impress at the top of the St Blazey and District Victory League after defeating Bethel ‘D’ 4-1 to stay three points clear of Bugle ‘A’.

more Ryan Tonkin, who made a personal best break of 62, which was a clearance in his doubles match last week continued his good form beating Leighton Varcoe but good wins for Chris Kendall, Phil Staley, Pete Chambers and Paul Williams keeps the Meva boys rolling on. Bugle ‘A’ also got four points at Pelynt for who Len Flower gave a real thrashing to Ian Poerll but there were easy wins for Andrew Best, Gary Spencer, Paul Sturtridge with Ian Ball sneaking past Will Courtis in the last. St Blazey ‘B’ played two matches on the same night. They lost out to Tregonissey ‘A’ by the odd frame. Ryan Bilson and Jason Simmons got Treggy’s first two frames with Mike Hancock and Jamie Read replying, with the result settled by Paul Baron going in off a respotted black to gift the win to Martin Knight. Blazey’s fortune got better against Bethel ‘A’. Baron picked up their first frame and it looked good for Jamie Read before Gary Stephens knocked in an excellent 39 break before going on to secure the frame. Mike Hancock and Graham Taylor put them 3-1 up only for Leon Collard got Bethel’s second point after beating Steve Clemo. St Blazey ‘A’ picked up a good 3-2 away win at Bugle ‘B’. Jake Moore secure his first win of the season by beating Vince Hathway only for Leigh Rosevear, Stewart Dewings and Chris Brown to put them 3-1 up with Mark Sturtridge making a 32 break in the finale against Nick Kendall. Mevagissey ‘B’ had a scare against their ‘D’ team but pulled through 3-2 to maintain their place at the top of the Rees League. Nathan Shaw and Ben Rothwell swapped frames with Rich Lakeman and Scott Mills which left a shootout between captains Mark Gregory and Rob Blamey which Mark secured, leaving them five points clear. St Stephen ‘A’ got a good 3-2 away win at Q-Bar to move up to third with a game in hand over the top two. Richie Bolt and Steve Bampton took the first two frames against Johnny Bennett and Shaun Hicks but excellent victories for Anthony Garside (Matt Woodward), Dan Allen (Marcus Causton) and Cary Clarke (Phil Ashton) saw the boys return home happy. Richard Holmes took the first frame for Gorran Haven at Mevagissey ‘C’ with Charlie Prynn levelling things. It looked good for Gorran as Lee McKinstry was well in control with a 30 plus lead over Joe Chesterfield but a stirling comeback was completed when Joe took the black to sneak home. Further wins for Dave ‘The Predator’ Tregale and Matt Chesterfield yielded a 4-1 victory, with the Predator completing a memorable week by knocking out Andy Peers of Bethel in the Bill Toms competition on Wednesday night. Lerryn went down 5-0 at Tregonissey ‘B’ but lost out on three occasions in black-ball frames. Michael Hooley and Ryan Thomas got the ball rolling then Sam Simon had a shaky first win of the season seeing off Charles McMiram with Phil Hooley and Gary Wherry completing the rout.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Pelynt 1 (Len Flower) Bugle ‘A’ 4 (Andrew Best, Gary Spencer, Paul Sturtridge, Ian Ball), Bugle ‘B’ 2 (Jake Moore, Mark Sturtridge (32)) St Blazey ‘A’ 3 (Leigh Rosevear, Stewart Dewings, Chris Brown), St Blazey ‘B’ 2 (Mike Hancock, Jamie Read) Tregonissey ‘A’ 3 (Ryan Bilson, Jason Simmonds, Martin Knight), Bethel ‘D’ 1 (Ryan Tonkin) Mevagissey ‘A’ 4 (Chris Kendall, Phil Staley, Pete Chambers, Paul Williams), Bethel ‘C’ 2 (Martin Powell, Paul Thomas) St Neot 3 (Anthony Maddams, Darren Lock, Steve Courts), St Blazey ‘B’ 3 (Paul Baron, Mike Hancock, Graham Taylor) Bethel ‘A’ 2 (Gary Stephens (39), Leon Collard). Rees Memorial League: Bodmin Pool 2 (Richard Bolt, Steve Bampton) St Stephen ‘A’ 3 (Anthony Garside, Dan Allen, Cary Clarke), Tregonissey ‘B’ 5 (Michael Hooley, Ryan Thomas, Sam Simon, Philip Hooley, Gary Wherry) Lerryn 0, Mevagissey ‘B’ 3 (Matt Rowe, Ben Rothwell, Mark Gregory) Mevagissey ‘D’ 2 (Rich Lakeman, Scott Mills), Mevagissey ‘C’ 4 (Charlie Prynn, Joe Chesterfield, Dave Tregale, Matt Chesterfield) Gorran Haven 1 (Richard Holmes). Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Top breaks of the week: M Sturtridge 32, G Stephens 39 Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Meva take a break beating', 'meva-take-a-break-beating', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Tywardreath ‘A’ went break crazy at home to Mevagissey ‘A’ as Darren Kirwan’s 38, Mike Hancock’s 30, Andrew Gooch’s 45, Paul Baron’s 38 and a 23 clearance from Lee Stutridge produced a 5-0 win.', null, '"The re-arranged Friday night duel between Lostwithiel ‘A’ and St Neot produced a 3-2 win for the visitors which now sees a three-way tie at the top of the league, in the closest season for many a year, with St Neot in fourth."', 'Tywardreath ‘A’ went break crazy at home to Mevagissey ‘A’ as Darren Kirwan’s 38, Mike Hancock’s 30, Andrew Gooch’s 45, Paul Baron’s 38 and a 23 clearance from Lee Stutridge produced a 5-0 win.

more There was a turn up for the books as Bugle ‘A’ triumphed 4-1 at Tregonissey with Barry McIntosh breaks of 51 and 33 over Andy Peers saved the whitewash a result which meant Treggy slipped to fifth in the table. Steve Allen marked his debut for Bethel ‘A’ with a fine 44 break as the hosts overcame Lostwithiel ‘B’ 4-1. The rearranged Friday night duel between Lostwithiel ‘A’ and St Neot produced a 3-2 win for the visitors which now sees a three-way tie at the top of the league, in the closest season for many a year, with St Neot in fourth. In the Rees League, Bethel ‘C’ have risen to fourth after a 5-0 beating of Gorran Haven, who were very unlucky not to pick up a couple of frames. Bethel ‘B’ remain undefeated and joined St Blazey on top of the league after a comprehensive 4-1 beating of Bugle ‘C’. Tywardreath ‘B’ continue to close in at the top winning 4-1 at Mevagissey ‘C’ with M Chesterfield stopping the whitewash and Lerryn moved off the bottom when gaining two points at home to Mevagissey ‘D’.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: To Follow Rees Memorial League: To Follow Top breaks of the week: Barry McIntosh 51 & 33, Andrew Gooch 45, Steve Allen 44, Paul Baron 38, Darren Kirwan 38, Mike Hancock 30, Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Meva''s crowning glory', 'mevas-crowning-glory', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Mevagissey ‘D’ clinched the Rees League title at Gorran with a 4-1 victory which could only be matched by nearest rivals Tregonissey ‘B’ for the Meva boys to win the title by the odd point.', null, 'Bethel ‘A’ went two down at home to Pelynt as John Daniell and Len Flower hammered Geoff Hawken and Gary Stephens with John having a 27 clearance for the second week running, but Bethel dug in and responded with wins for Matt Green and Andy Peers leaving Leon Collard’s black ball win over Phil Ormand getting the victory.', 'Mevagissey ‘D’ clinched the Rees League title at Gorran with a 4-1 victory which could only be matched by nearest rivals Tregonissey ‘B’ for the Meva boys to win the title by the odd point.

more Lee McKinstry beat Rob Blamey in the opener to get Gorran off to a good start but there was big wins for George Lory, Ian Latham, Rich Lakeman and Mark Ashton who knocked in breaks of 40 and 36 when defeating Andy Reddall as the Meva boys celebrated. Treggy ‘B’ needed a full hand against Bethel ‘D’ to have any chance of the title and Phil Hooley’s 30 break closely followed by son Justin’s win put them 2-0 up. but Andy Spearman beat Simon Garrington in the third frame to knock them back. Mike Hooley and Richard Opie completed a 4-1 win but they came up just short after a very good season. Bethel ‘C’ were pushed all the way by the ladies of Tregonissey ‘C’ but got through by the odd frame. Great wins for Michaela Edwards and Stacey Hancock over Courtney Crockford and Leigh Rosevear had put them two up but Roger Smithson and Ben Stunnell followed up the earlier win for Howard Brett for Bethel to squeeze home. Lerryn jumped up a couple of places in the league thanks to a great 4-1 win at St Stephen ‘B’. James Stephens, Charles McMiram, Terry Middleton and John Hancock were the winners with Terry Nicholls getting past Graham Hoskin for the home point. Runners up spot in the Victory League will go to the wire with Bugle ‘A’ needing four points at Mevagissey ‘A’ last night to claim second place. Bethel ‘A’ went two down at home to Pelynt as John Daniell and Len Flower hammered Geoff Hawken and Gary Stephens with John having a 27 clearance for the second week running, but Bethel dug in and responded with wins for Matt Green and Andy Peers leaving Leon Collard’s black ball win over Phil Ormand getting the victory. Adrian Bowyer’s great win over Nigel Couchman stopped Bugle ‘A’s full house against St Stephen. Big wins for Gary Spencer, Paul Sturtidge, Andrew Best and Ian Ball leaves Bugle knowing exactly what they needed last night with Bethel’s campaign completed. Bugle ‘B’s 3-2 win at St Neot condemned Mevagissey ‘B’ to the Rees League. Steve Courts took the opener beating Keith Moore but Mark Sturtridge and Dave Rundle put Bethel in the lead. Darren Lock levelled things up beating Colin Rundle only for Tony Conroy to hammer Chris Perring in the last for a good victory. Paul Williams’ 40 and Jame Popplewell’s 39 breaks helped Mevagissey ‘A’ get a good 3-2 win at St Blazey ‘A’. Phil Staley got Meva’s other point with Stewart Dewings and Nick Kendall replying.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bugle ‘A’ 4 (G Spencer, P Sturtridge, A Best, I Ball) St Stephen ‘A’ 1 (A Bowyer), St Blazey ‘A’ 2 (S Dewings, N Kendall) Mevagissey ‘D’ 3 (P Williams (40), P Staley, J Popplwell (39)), Bethel ‘A’ 3 (M Green, A Peers, L Collard) Pelynt 2 (L Flower, J Daniell), St Neot 2 (S Courts, D Lock) Bugle ‘B’ 3 (M Sturtridge, D Rundle, T Conroy). Bethel ‘C’ 3 (B Stunnell, R Smithson, H Brett) Tregonissey ‘C’ 2 (M Edwards, S Hancock), Gorran 1 (L McKinstry) Mevagissey ‘D’ 4 (G Lory, M Ashton (40+36), I Latham, R Lakeman), Tregonissey ‘B’ 4 (P Hooley, J Hooley, M Hooley, R Opie) Bethel ‘D’ 1 (A Spearman), St Stephen ‘B’ 1 (T Nicholls) Lerryn 4 (J Stephens, C McMiram, T Middleton, J Hancock). Top breaks of the week: J Popplewell 39, P Williams 40 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Mevagissey teams leading the way', 'mevagissey-teams-leading-the-way', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Pete Chambers secured a 32 break in the final frame of the evening against Martin Knight as leaders Mevagissey ‘A’ kept their lead at the top of the Victory League.', null, 'St Stephen ‘B’ picked up two useful points at home to Mevagissey ‘C’. Ben and Charlie Prynn along with Joe Chesterfield took Meva’s point but Rob Garside and Josh Doherty sneaked home by the odd point with victories over Matt Chesterfield and the predator Tregale.', 'Pete Chambers secured a 32 break in the final frame of the evening against Martin Knight as leaders Mevagissey ‘A’ kept their lead at the top of the Victory League.

more Wins for two Jasons – Simmons and Cocks had put Treggy ‘A’ 2-1 up but Matt Mills followed up captain Staley’s win for the leaders to stay unbeaten. Bugle ‘A’ have jumped to equal second after thrashing Bethel ‘A’ 5-0. Gary Stephens and Leon Collard had early chances to put points on the board for the home side but overall the result was a fair one with Andy Best knocking in a 31 break whilst beating Andy Peers. Bugle ‘B’ have put a healthy gap between themselves and Bethel ‘C’ and ‘D’ after beating the former 4-1. There were fairly even wins for Mark Sturtridge, Jake and Keith Moore and Dave Rundle secured a black ball win over Howard Brett. Early Martin Powell beat Colin Rundle for Bethel’s point. St Blazey ‘B’ finished their pre-Christmas games beating Pelynt 4-1. Richard Broad beat Graham Taylor for Pelynt’s point but Mike Hancock’s 38 and Jamie Read’s 33 with wins also for Paul Baron and Dave Grose saw Blazey take the honours. Anything that Mevagissey ‘A’ can do their ‘B’ can do just as well as they stayed unbeaten too after getting a full house at Tregonissey ‘B’ and they will be taking a healthy lead into the Christmas break. Dave Kendall and Matt Rowe won the first two on the black followed by good wins for Ben Rothwell, Mark Gregory and Nathan Shaw. Mevagissey ‘D’ had a great 3-2 home win over St Stephen ‘A’. All the frames were tight on the night with Rich Lakeman, George Lory and Scott Mills the home winners with Anthony Garside and still unbeaten Dan Allen replying. St Stephen ‘B’ picked up two useful points at home to Mevagissey ‘C’. Ben and Charlie Prynn along with Joe Chesterfield took Meva’s point but Rob Garside and Josh Doherty sneaked home by the odd point with victories over Matt Chesterfield and the predator Tregale. The league are very sorry that Lerryn stalwart Dave Phipps passed away last week. A hugely likeable, pleasant gentleman, who made many friends in the league over the years will be greatly missed and everyone’s thoughts are with his family.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bethel ‘A’ 0 Bugle ‘B’ 5 (Paul Stuthridge, Ian Powell, Ian Ball, Andrew Best (31), Gary Spencer), Mevagissey ‘A’ 3 (Phil Staley, Matt Mills, Pete Chambers (32)) Tregonissey ‘A’ 2 (Jason Simmons, Jason Cocks), Bugle ‘B’ 4 (Mark Sturtridge, Jake Moore, Keith Moore, Dave Rundle) Bethel ‘C’ 1 (Martin Powell), St Blazey ‘B’ 4 (Paul Baron, Mike Hancock (38), Jamie Read (33), David Grose) Pelynt 1 (Richard Broad). Rees Memorial League: Mevagissey ‘D’ 3 (Richard Lakeman, George Lory, Scott Mills) St Stephen ‘A’ 2 (Anthony Garside, Dan Allen), St Stephen ‘B’ 2 (Rob Garside, Josh Doherty) Mevagissey ‘C’ 3 (Ben Prynn, Charlie Prynn, Joe Chesterfield), Tregonissey ‘B’ 0 Mevagissey ‘B’ 5 (Dave Kendall, Matt Rowe, Ben Rothwell, Mark Gregory, Nathan Shaw). Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Top breaks of the week: A Best 31, P Chambers 32, M Hancock 38, J Read 33 Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Meva’s 10th straight loss as Treggy close in on title', 'mevas-10th-straight-loss-as-treggy-close-in-on-title', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Tregonissey ‘A’ march on at the the top of the Victory League after a convincing 4-1 win over Mevagissey ‘B’ who slipped to second bottom after this their 10th consecutive defeat.', null, 'Tregonissey ‘C’ got three points at Bethel ‘B’ through Simon Garrington, Mike Hooley and Richard Opie, who knocked in a fine 35 break. Bethel’s winners were Clive Stuthridge and Jim Tamblyn, who beat Justin and Phil Hooley respectively.', 'Tregonissey ‘A’ march on at the the top of the Victory League after a convincing 4-1 win over Mevagissey ‘B’ who slipped to second bottom after this their 10th consecutive defeat.

more Jason Simmons knocked in a 31 break in the opener followed by Barrie McIntosh’s 44 in the second with Ryan Bilson and Jason Cocks putting them four up. Meva’s reply came from Dave Kendall who took out Martin Knight in the last frame. Bethel ‘A’ remain second after a 4-1 win at St Stephen ‘A’ who are all but relegated. Wins for Paul Baron, Geoff Hawken and Matt Green put them three up only for Dan Allen to beat Andy Pears to pull one back with Dave Patton getting Bethel’s second point beating Anthony Harrison. Bugle ‘A’ dropped two points to Pelynt and only victory at Treggy last night would keep the in the title hunt. Andy Parnell got Pelynt off to a great start beating Paul Sturtridge, Jake Moore levelled things up and with Andrew Best with 56 and Ian Ball’s 38 things looked good only for Phil Ormand to beat Gary Spencer for the second away point. Bugle ‘B’ moved out of the relegation places despite a 3-2 defeat at St Blazey ‘A’. Colin Rundle beat Mark Craddock but Mickey Meares levelled by getting Mark Sturtridge. Tony Conroy got Bugle’s second point but Nick Kendall squeezed past Dave Rundle to level it up again with Stewart Dewings beating Keith Moore in the decider. It was a good evening for non-playing leaders of the Rees League as their challengers dropped points. Tregonissey ‘C’ got three points at Bethel ‘B’ through Simon Garrington, Mike Hooley and Richard Opie, who knocked in a fine 35 break. Bethel’s winners were Clive Stuthridge and Jim Tamblyn, who beat Justin and Phil Hooley respectively. St Stephen ‘B’ won two black ball games at Bethel ‘C’, through Terry Nicholls and Darren Biggen with wins for the home side for Ben Stunnell, Howard Brett and Roger Smithson. Leaving them needing a very good result at Mevagissey to keep their promotion hopes alive. Tregonissey ‘C’ got two welcome points at home to Mevagissey ‘C’ with Caz Stephens beating the Predator Tregale and Amanda Fle,ing getting the better of Joe Chesterfield. Meva’s points came from Charlie Prynn, Matt Chesterfield and Adrian Evans, David Davis put Lerryn one up at Gorran but the home side responded through Lee McKinstry and Rob Bunney only for Graham Hoskin’s defeating Andy Reddall to level up, leaving Terry Middleton’s victory over Paul Grimmett to settle the night.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bugle ‘A’ 3 (J Moore, A Best (56), I Ball) Pelynt 2 (A Parnell, P Ormand), St Blazey ‘A’ 3 (M Meares, N Kendall, S Dewings) Bugle ‘B’ 2 (C Rundle, T Conroy), Tregonissey ‘A’ 4 (J Simmons (31), B McIntosh (44), R Bilson, J Cocks) Mevagissey ‘B’ 1 (D Kendall), St Stephen ‘A’ 1 (D Allen) Bethel ‘A’ 4 (P Baron, G Hawken, M Green, D Patton). Rees Memorial League: Bethel ‘C’ 3 (B Stunnell, H Brett, R Smithson) St Stephen ‘B’ 2 (C Nicholls, D Biggen), Gorran 2 (L McKinstry, R Bunney) Lerryn 3 (D Davis, G Hoskins, T Middleton), Tregonissey ‘C’ 2 (C Stephens, A Fleming) Mevagissey ‘C’ 3 (C Prynn, M Chesterfield, A Evans), Bethel ‘B’ 2 (C Stuthridge, J Tamblyn) Tregonissey ‘B’ 3 (S Garrington, M Hooley, R Opie (35)). Top breaks of the week: A Best 56, B McIntosh 44, J Simmons 31, R Opie 35 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Mighty Meva still top of the pots', 'mighty-meva-still-top-of-the-pots', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Mevagissey ‘A’ stay top of the Victory League despite the odd-frame defeat at Bethel ‘A’.', null, 'It all started so well for the visitors as Richard Lakeman beaten Philip Hooley but easy wins for Mark Sullivan, Richard Opie and Mike Hooley followed by Justin Hooley’s black-ball win against George Lory ensured a fine victory.', 'Mevagissey ‘A’ stay top of the Victory League despite the odd-frame defeat at Bethel ‘A’.

more Leighton Varcoe beat Garry Stephens to get Meva underway but Andy Peers (Phil Staley), Matt Green (Matt Mills) and Leon Collard (James Popperwell) put the home side 3-1 up but Rich Lakeman’s victory over Geoff Hawken secured Meva’s second point. Tregonissey ‘A’ returned from St Neot with a 3-2 win. Matt Woodward opened up with a victory over Cyril Dickenson but Chris Oerring and Anthony Maddams replied. Barrie McIntosh’s win over Darren Lock levelled things up with Ryan Bilson’s 89-13 win over Steve Courts deciding the contest. Pelynt produced a fine 4-1 home win over St Stephen ‘A’ for their first win getting the better of two very close frames between Richard Broad (Brian Green) and John Daniell (Dan Allen). Len Flower had earlier defeated John Bennett but Andrew Gooch beat Phil Ormand on the black to get the visitors’ black to get the visitors point. Graham Taylor and Stewart Dewings got St Blazey ‘A’ off to a flier at Mevagissey ‘B’. Matt Rowe replied beating Aiden Craddock. Mickey Meares’ good run continued as he saw off Dave Kendall with Ben Rothwell getting the better of Mark Craddock to secure Meva’s second point. In a re-arranged game on Friday night St Neot came from 2-0 down to defeat Bethel ‘A’. Geoff Hawken and Matt Green beat Steve Courts and Cyril Dickenson respectively but Darren Lock’s fine 51 break saw him past Gary Stephens with Anthony Maddams and Chris Perring completing the comeback. Tregonissey ‘B’ stay top and unbeaten after a 4-1 home win over Mevagissey ‘D’. It all started so well for the visitors as Richard Lakeman beaten Philip Hooley but easy wins for Mark Sullivan, Richard Opie and Mike Hooley followed by Justin Hooley’s black-ball win against George Lory ensured a fine victory. In the Bethel derby between the B and D teams. Ross McMenemy and Richard Bolt traded the opening two frames. Jason Pascoe restored the ‘D’ team’s lead only for Jim Tamblyn and Clive Stuthridge to seal it for the ‘B’ who remain in second. Bethel ‘C’ jump up to third place with a 4-1 win at Lerryn. Rich Allen, Leigh Rosevear, Courtney Crockford and Martin Powell got their points with Terry Middleton beating Roger Smithson in the battle of the captains, for Lerryn’s point. Tregonissey ‘C’ started well at St Stephen ‘B’ with wins for Stacey Hancock and Amanda Fleming but Rob Garside along with Terry and Craig Nicholls sealed a home victory. Gorran Haven made the short trip to Mevagissey ‘C’ and produced a fine 4-1 win. Rob Bunney, Lee McKinstry, Paul Grimmett and Matt Fox were the marksmen with Ben Prynn replying. All competition draws are on the league website.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Mevagissey ‘B’ 2 (M Rowe, B Rothwell) St Blazey ‘A’ 3 (M Meares, S Dewings, G Taylor), Bethel ‘A’ 3 (A Peers, L Collard, M Green) Mevagissey ‘A’ 2 (L Varcoe, R Lakeman), St Neot 2 (C Perring, A Maddams) Tregonissey ‘A’ 3 (M Woodward, B McIntosh, R Bilson), Pelynt 4 (L Flower, A Parnell, R Broad, J Daniell) St Stephen ‘A’ 1 (A Gooch), St Neot 3 (D Lock (51), A Maddams, C Perring) Bethel ‘A’ 2 (G Hawken, M Green). Rees Memorial League: Lerryn 1 (T Middleton) Bethel ‘C’ 4 (L Rosevear, C Crockford, R Allen, M Powell), Mevagissey 1 (B Prynn) Gorran Haven 4 (R Bunney, L McKinstry, P Grimmett, M Fox), Tregonissey ‘B’ 4 (M Sullivan, R Opie, M Hooley, J Hooley) Mevagissey ‘D’ 1 (R Lakeman), St Stephen ‘B’ 3 (R Garside, T Nicholls, C Nicholls) Tregonissey ‘C’ 2 (S Hancock, A Fleming), Bethel ‘D’ 2 (R McMenemy, J Pascoe) Bethel ‘B’ 3 (C Stuthridge, R Bolt, J Tamblyn). Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Top breaks of the week: J Popperwell 39, Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Mixed week for Tregonissey', 'mixed-week-for-tregonissey', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Tregonissey suffered a surprise home defeat to Bugle ‘B’ in their re-arranged game on Monday night as Jake and Keith Moore and Ian Ball surprised their hosts but Treggy bounced back on Tuesday night defeating…', null, '"The form book went out the door at Bodmin Sparks as Mevagissey ‘C’ claimed a brilliant 4-1 victory and with a game in hand at least on all above they look a real force in the division, Bert Bampton saved the whitewash for the Sparkies."', 'Tregonissey suffered a surprise home defeat to Bugle ‘B’ in their re-arranged game on Monday night as Jake and Keith Moore and Ian Ball surprised their hosts but Treggy bounced back on Tuesday night defeating Lostwithiel ‘A’ 4-1 to cement third place, with Darren Lock gaining Lostwithiel’s point.

more Bugle ‘A’ were taken aback as Andy Spearman beat Andy Best, but they recovered to record a 4-1 victory over Bethel ‘D which gave them a five-point lead going into last night’s massive match at second-placed St Blazey ‘B’ who came away from Lostwithiel ‘B’ with a full house. St Blazey ‘A’ raced into a 3-0 home lead over St Neot but were pulled back by Callum Lundon and Steve Courts but stay a comfortable fourth place and it was nearly a case of after the Lord Mayor’s Show for Leighton Varcoe as he trailed Gary Stephens by 38 points, deep into the frame at Bethel ‘A’ but produced some magical shots and flukes to win and help his side secure a 3-2 win, ably assisted by Chris Kendall and Phil Staley. The form book went out the door at Bodmin Sparks as Mevagissey ‘C’ claimed a brilliant 4-1 victory and with a game in hand at least on all above they look a real force in the division, Bert Bampton saved the whitewash for the Sparkies. Bethel ‘C’ remain top of the league as only Nigel Couchman registered for Bugle ‘C’ as the Bethel boys continue in fine fettle and their ‘B’ side jumped into second by despatching Gorran Haven 4-1 with Rob Bunney seeing off Mike Sweet in the final frame of the evening. Graham Hoskin and Gordon Brimicombe picked up two valuable points for Lerryn at Mevagissey ‘B’ who will be disappointed not to have moved nearer to the top in the league which looks impossible to call at present.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bethel ‘A’ 2 (L Collard, G Hawken) Mevagissey ‘A’ 3 (L Varcoe, P Staley, C Kendall), Bugle ‘A’ 4 (G Spencer, A Peers, P Sturtridge, I Powell) Bethel ‘D’ 1 (A Spearman), Loswithiel ‘B’ 0 St Blazey ‘B’ 5 (D Kirwan, P Baron, G Taylor, B Phillips, N Hancock (37)), St Blazey ‘A’ 3 (S Dewings, C Brown, M Woodward (30)) St Neot 2 (C Lundon, S Courts), Tregonissey 4 (B McIntosh, J Cocks, R Bilson (34), C Stephenson) Lostwithiel ‘A’ 1 (D Lock), Tregonnisey 2 (B McIntosh, R Bilson) Bugle ‘B’ 3 (J Moore, I Ball, K Moore). Rees Memorial League: Bugle ‘C’ 1 (N Couchman) Bethel ‘C’ 4 (M Powell, C Crockford, H Brett, R Smithson), Mevagissey ‘B’ 3 (B Rothwell, M Gregory, D Kendall) Lerryn 2 (G Hoskins, G Brimicombe), Sparks 1 (S Bampton) Mevagissey ‘C’ 4 (M Chesterfield, B Prynn, J Chesterfield, D Tregale), Bethel ‘B’ 4 (S Hicks, M Green, J Tamblyn, G Buckle) Gorran Haven 1 (R Bunney). Top breaks of the week: N Hancock 37, R Bilson 34 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Murphy Rules The Rock', 'murphy-rules-the-rock', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Shaun Murphy won his first ranking title of the season by beating Judd Trump 4-2 in the final of the Jojobet.com Gibraltar Open.', null, '“This is the first time I have come to Gibraltar and I have really enjoyed it. Everyone has been fantastic, we have had great support and I’m looking forward to coming back next year.”', 'Shaun Murphy won his first ranking title of the season by beating Judd Trump 4-2 in the final of the Jojobet.com Gibraltar Open.

more Murphy won the seventh ranking title of his career – and first since the World Grand Prix 12 months ago – with a fine display against Trump in a high quality final. Nottingham-based 34-year-old Murphy won six matches in the tournament to capture the 25,000 Euro top prize. Defeat was another blow for Bristol’s Trump as he lost 9-8 to Stuart Bingham in the Welsh Open final last month. The left-hander is still searching for his seventh ranking title and has now lost eight of his 14 finals. Trump won the opening frame tonight with a break of 59 before Murphy levelled with a 46 clearance. Trump’s 84 put him 2-1 up then Murphy made a 60 in recovering to 2-2. Both players made a break of 56 in frame five but it was Murphy who took it by clearing from yellow to pink. A thumping long red from Trump early in frame six set him up for a chance to force a decider, but he missed a short range red to a top corner on 34. Murphy took advantage with a superb 96 to secure the trophy. “I had a few tough matches during the tournament and that prepared me for the final,” said Murphy. “There are a few of us who think that Judd is the best player in the world at the moment, he is strong in every department. So I knew I would have to play well to beat him. “This is the first time I have come to Gibraltar and I have really enjoyed it. Everyone has been fantastic, we have had great support and I’m looking forward to coming back next year.” Trump added: “I had enough chances but I didn’t take them. Shaun punished me and he deserved to win because he was the best player of the tournament.” Earlier in the semi-finals, Trump saw off Ryan Day 4-2 while Murphy made breaks of 140 and 137 in a 4-0 win over veteran Nigel Bond. Article & Images courtesy of the World Snooker', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Murphy Storms To Bulgaria Title', 'murphy-storms-to-bulgaria-title', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'World number seven Murphy played superb snooker throughout the European Tour event in the Bulgarian capital, conceding just seven frames in seven matches on his way to the 25,000 Euro top prize.', null, '"I''ve been coming to Bulgaria for a few years now and I know how popular snooker is here. The fans are fantastic - they love the sport and I can''t wait to come back next year."', 'World number seven Murphy played superb snooker throughout the European Tour event in the Bulgarian capital, conceding just seven frames in seven matches on his way to the 25,000 Euro top prize.

more After a barren spell of nearly three years, Murphy won the Gdynia Open and Haikou World Open last season, and now has his first title of the 2014/15 campaign. Former World and UK Champion Murphy dominated the opening frame before Gould took the next two with breaks of 60 and 45. But Murphy then stepped up a gear and fired in breaks of 89, 99 and 63 to win the last three frames in style. "Most of the matches this week have gone my way all the way through, but Martin really pushed me hard," said Manchester-based 32-year-old Murphy. "At 2-1 I had to get myself sorted. I played really well after that. "It has been a brilliant year for me so far. I''ve been working very hard on my game and it''s really nice to see the fruits of that. I''m a much better player now than I was when I won the world title nearly ten years ago. Hopefully there''s more to come. I''m going to keep putting the hours in on the practice table and going for it. There will be no burn-out for me. "I''ve been coming to Bulgaria for a few years now and I know how popular snooker is here. The fans are fantastic - they love the sport and I can''t wait to come back next year." Earlier in the semi-finals, Murphy beat Michael White 4-0 while Gould saw off Peter Ebdon 4-2. The 2014/15 European Tour series comprises six events across the continent, with the top 24 in the final Order or Merit to go through to the Players Championship next March to battle for the £100,000 jackpot. The next event is the Ruhr Open in Germany from November 19 to 23. Article courtesy of the World Snooker Images Courtesy of: Monique Limbos', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Murphy The Master', 'murphy-the-master', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Shaun Murphy crushed Neil Robertson 10-2 in the final of the Dafabet Masters to complete the Triple Crown of snooker''s three biggest titles.', null, '""It hasn''t hit me yet at all, it will take days, weeks and months for what I have achieved to sink in," said Murphy. "The media has made up the idea of the Triple Crown and it really does affect the players who have won two out of the three. I won my first one and wanted more, then I got my second and I''ve had to wait seven years for the third. It''s been a long time coming, there has been a lot of hard work and I''m absolutely blown away."', 'Shaun Murphy crushed Neil Robertson 10-2 in the final of the Dafabet Masters to complete the Triple Crown of snooker''s three biggest titles.

more In the most one-sided Masters final since Steve Davis beat Mike Hallett 9-0 in 1988, Murphy completely outplayed his opponent with a superb display at Alexandra Palace. He made two centuries and four more breaks over 50, and won the crucial tight frames which kept his opponent grounded. Robertson had played some of the best snooker of his career in 6-1 wins over Ali Carter and Ronnie O''Sullivan to reach the final, but could not reproduce that today, struggling with every department of his game. Manchester-based Murphy took the top prize of £200,000, but to join a group of just nine other players to have won all three of snooker''s biggest titles will mean much more to the 32-year-old. The 2005 World and 2008 UK Champion now joins Steve Davis, Terry Griffiths, Alex Higgins, Stephen Hendry, John Higgins, Mark Williams, Ronnie O''Sullivan, Neil Robertson and Mark Selby in that elite club. It''s a remarkable turn-around for Murphy, who sank to one of the lowest points of his career a year ago when he lost 6-1 to Mark Selby in the Masters semi-finals. Without a title of any sort in two and a half years, he knew he was in danger of failing to fulfil his potential. It had been nine years since he burst on to the scene by winning the World Championship at the age of 22 as a dazzling young talent. But he had since added just one other BBC-televised title - the 2008 UK - and was seen by many as an under-achiever. Murphy even suggested he was considering a career away from snooker - albeit in the haze of disappointment following that Selby defeat. But 2014 turned out to be one of the best years of his career as he won three European Tour events as well as the Haikou World Open, and also became the first player ever to make three 147s in a calendar year. The turn-around is a result of countless hours of hard work on the practice table, particularly on his break-building; he made five centuries and 11 more breaks over 50 in the 28 frames he won this week. World number 11 Murphy has also found contentment away from the table and was watched live for the first time this week by fiancée Elaine and his mother. World number one Robertson missed out on his second Masters title, having beaten Murphy in the 2012 final. The Australian also lost to Selby here in the 2013 final. Murphy led 6-2 after the first session, and won a vital 34-minute opening frame tonight to extend his lead. Robertson made a break of 51 and had several chances to seal the frame, but couldn''t take one and it came down to the last two balls. A safety error from Robertson, leaving the pink close to a baulk corner, allowed Murphy to pot pink and black. From that moment a Robertson fight-back never looked likely, indeed he scored just 20 points in the remainder of the match. Murphy made a 127 in frame ten, dominated the 11th for 9-2 and clinched the title in the next with a calm run of 60. "It hasn''t hit me yet at all, it will take days, weeks and months for what I have achieved to sink in," said Murphy. "The media has made up the idea of the Triple Crown and it really does affect the players who have won two out of the three. I won my first one and wanted more, then I got my second and I''ve had to wait seven years for the third. It''s been a long time coming, there has been a lot of hard work and I''m absolutely blown away. "At 5-0 I was only half way, and Neil has given me some thumpings over the years so I knew he was more than capable of coming back. The first frame tonight was important because it went on for a while and there was a bit of tension. Tactically I have improved - two or three years ago I could never have won that frame against Neil. "It was an unbelievable feeling to pot the last few balls. To win this playing good snooker is very pleasing. This time 12 months ago I talked about giving up snooker and going off to do something else. I stuck at it and I''ve lived in the snooker club since, and it has finally paid off. There are plenty more trophies out there to be won." Robertson said: "In the first couple of frames I had chances and it should at least have been 1-1. The match just seemed an uphill struggle from there. He was playing very well and I wasn''t at the same level as I was against Carter and O''Sullivan. I was bound to come down after those two matches because I had to play so well to win them. The first session finished 6-2 when it could have been 4-4, then I could have won the first frame tonight. Shaun played a fantastic match, but I still had the chances to be well in it. "My cue ball control today wasn''t anywhere near as good as it had been in previous matches. I''m really disappointed to have played like that in a big final but sometimes you have to take it on the chin and move on to the next one. "It''s very fitting that a player as good as Shaun has won all three majors. I did it, then Mark Selby did it, and now we are all trying to win multiple Triple Crown events. A defeat like this motivates me to practice more." Article courtesy of the World Snooker Images Courtesy of: Tai Chengzhe', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Murphy Wins In Mulheim – And Makes 147', 'murphy-wins-in-mulheim-and-makes-147', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Shaun Murphy won the Kreative Dental Ruhr Open in Germany, making a maximum break in a 4-0 victory over Robert Milkins in the final.', null, '"I had a few barren years where I started to lose a bit of faith in my game. I was looking for answers elsewhere and I even thought about jacking it in. I''m really pleased that I stuck with it and got through that bad patch, and I''m so happy to have won another trophy."', 'Shaun Murphy won the Kreative Dental Ruhr Open in Germany, making a maximum break in a 4-0 victory over Robert Milkins in the final.

more Murphy made it back-to-back European Tour titles having won last month''s Bulgarian Open and the world number seven has now won four titles in 2014. The highlight of his emphatic final win over Milkins in Mulheim came in the second frame as he potted 15 reds with blacks and cleared the colours to score the 108th maximum break in snooker history. It was the fourth 147 of Murphy''s career and third of 2014. He becomes the first player to make three maximums in a calendar year, having made one at the Championship League in January and another at the Gdynia Open in February. Amazingly, Murphy had missed two golden chances of 147s earlier in the day, getting as far as the colours both times but missing the blue in his last 16 match against Joe Swail and the green in his quarter-final against Mark Williams. Manchester-based 32-year Murphy collected 25,000 Euros for winning the tournament, plus a £1,000 bonus for the maximum. He also compiled breaks of 66, 62 and 58 in a one-sided final against Gloucester''s Milkins, who had hoped to win a tournament carrying ranking points for the first time in his 19-year career. Former World Champion Murphy, who goes into next week''s Coral UK Championship in peak form, said: "I had four or five chances at maximums before the final. When I got the chance tonight I was under so much pressure. I potted a very difficult 15th black and finished perfect on the yellow, then I was just concentrating on every ball. "When I started working with my coach Chris Henry we focussed on making 147s because top players make them all the time but I wasn''t doing that. Now the work I''ve done is coming through. "I had a few barren years where I started to lose a bit of faith in my game. I was looking for answers elsewhere and I even thought about jacking it in. I''m really pleased that I stuck with it and got through that bad patch, and I''m so happy to have won another trophy. "We all came here this week to prepare for the UK Championship and I can''t wait to get stuck in next week." Earlier in the semi-finals, Murphy came from 3-0 down to beat Judd Trump 4-3 while Milkins saw off Stephen Maguire 4-1. The 2014/15 European Tour series comprises six events across the continent, with the top 24 in the final Order or Merit to go through to the Players Championship next March to battle for the £100,000 jackpot. The next event is the Lisbon Open in Portugal from December 11 to 14. Results (last 16 onwards): Last 16 Rod Lawler 4-3 Stuart Carrington Robert Milkins 4-2 Andrew Higginson Neil Robertson 4-3 Liang Wenbo Stephen Maguire 4-1 Stuart Bingham Judd Trump 4-3 Marco Fu Chris Wakelin 4-2 Fergal O''Brien Mark Williams 4-3 Mark King Shaun Murphy 4-3 Joe Swail Quarter-finals Milkins 4-3 Lawler Maguire 4-2 Robertson Trump 4-1 Wakelin Murphy 4-2 Williams Semi-finals Milkins 4-1 Maguire Murphy 4-3 Trump Final Murphy 4-0 Milkins Article courtesy of the World Snooker Images Courtesy of: Tai Chengzhe', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Murphy captures World Grand Prix', 'murphy-captures-world-grand-prix', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Shaun Murphy won a dramatic final 10-9 against Stuart Bingham to win his first ranking title for two years at the Ladbrokes World Grand Prix in Llandudno.', null, '“There are three events left this season and hopefully more good things for me. There’s nothing like the feeling of taking trophies home. My six ranking titles have taken some winning and I feel I’m on the right road to win more.”', 'Shaun Murphy won a dramatic final 10-9 against Stuart Bingham to win his first ranking title for two years at the Ladbrokes World Grand Prix in Llandudno.

more An enthralling contest swung one way then the other throughout the day and was only settled when Murphy got the better of a tense deciding frame. The 33-year-old captured the sixth ranking title of his career and first since the 2014 Haikou World Open, as well as the £100,000 top prize. The match was a repeat of last season’s World Championship final when Bingham came from 8-4 down to win 18-15. Today’s battle brought back memories of that Crucible crash as Murphy let slip an early lead, but this time it had a different outcome as the Nottingham-based potter ended up with the silverware. Murphy moves level with Ken Doherty and Mark Selby on the all-time list of ranking event winners and climbs to number four in the world. Until this week it had been a disappointing season for Murphy, his only other final coming at the Paul Hunter Classic European Tour event when he lost to Ali Carter. He admits he tinkered with his tactics and technique after losing the world final, and only recently went back to his natural style of all-out attack. That paid dividends in North Wales as he conceded just four frames en route to the final and eventually secured the trophy. Bingham has also struggled for form for much of the season but can take confidence from reaching his first final of the campaign this week. The world number two goes back to Basildon with a cheque for £35,000 and can look forward to the defence of his world crown in Sheffield next month. Leading 5-4 after the first session, Bingham soon extended his lead tonight with a break of 68. At that stage he had won five frames in a row and he looked set to go 7-4 ahead until he missed a red to a top corner on 40 and Murphy stayed in touch with a cool 52 clearance. Runs of 37 and 35 got Murphy back to 6-6 then Bingham regained the lead in frame 13 with a break of 45 after trapping his opponent in a tough snooker. Murphy hit back after the interval with a 94. In frame 15, Murphy made 48 before running out of position with three reds left. Bingham had a chance to counter but, having made just 8, suffered a stroke of misfortune when his attempted black bounced off the back off a top corner pocket and stayed on the baize. That left Murphy able to pot the last red to go 8-7 ahead. Frame 16 lasted 32 minutes and came down to the colours. Bingham laid a snooker on the yellow, and from the chance that followed he cleared the table to square the match. A missed red from Bingham early in the next proved costly as Murphy made a tremendous 120, the only century of the contest. He had first chance in frame 18 but potted just one red before missing the pink to a top corner. Bingham’s runs of 23 and 38 put him ahead and, after Murphy missed the pink again to a centre pocket, he added 22 for 9-9. Murphy had first chance in the decider after a thumping long red and made 24 before running out of position. After a safety exchange, he missed a tricky red to a top corner, then Bingham rattled a red in the jaws of the same pocket. Another brief safety tussle was ended when Murphy cracked a fantastic long red to a baulk corner, and this time there was no mistake as he added 47 points for victory. “It had the feel of a 9-9 all day,” said 2005 World Champion Murphy. “I was trying to go for my shots and make something happen, but Stuart played some good snooker. At 4-1 I was in the balls and had the chance to pull away, but didn’t take it and he came back at me. At 5-4 down my head was gone and I was just happy to get out of the arena. Stuart was very unlucky at 7-7 when the black bounced out, I think it hit the lens of the TV pocket camera. It took me 30 seconds to realise what had happened. At 9-9 I made a mess of one chance but then went for a long red and got it. “There are three events left this season and hopefully more good things for me. There’s nothing like the feeling of taking trophies home. My six ranking titles have taken some winning and I feel I’m on the right road to win more.” Bingham said: “I struggled all day and I was lucky to have a chance to win in the end. At 4-1 I felt I was playing with someone else’s arm, so I was ecstatic to finish 5-4 up this afternoon. I had a chance to go 7-4 but got a kick on a red and that changed the final because I was in a good rhythm. “I couldn’t believe it when the black jumped out of the pocket at 7-7, I’ve only ever seen that once or twice before. In the last frame I just wanted a chance. I put all my eggs in one basket with the red along the top cushion but it didn’t go in and he potted a great long red.My B game has kept me in matches this week so when my A game arrives I’ll be in good shape. I can take a lot of positives from this week.” Article & Images courtesy of the World Snooker', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('My Life - By Andy Hicks (parts 1& 2)', 'my-life-by-andy-hicks-parts-1-2', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Here at St Blazey Snooker online, we are always on the lookout for new articles and exclusive features. I can rightly say that we are honoured to be getting a huge input from Snooker Professional and qualified coach…', null, '"Dad used to take me down every wednesday evening to the pot black club where i would play Andy Snell who was probably in the top ten of the amateur rankings at the time. Great great player who was slow and methodical. We would play 6 til 8 and manage to get about 6 frames in if we were lucky where he would beat me 5 to 6 nil every week with out fail.".', 'Here at St Blazey Snooker online, we are always on the lookout for new articles and exclusive features. I can rightly say that we are honoured to be getting a huge input from Snooker Professional and qualified coach Andy Hicks.

more Andy has agreed to write a series of articles documenting his career, his thoughts and life behind the scenes as a snooker professional. We caught up with him recently at his cluv in Crackington Haven, North Cornwall to speak with him and get an EXCLUSIVE photoshoot. (below at foot of page)

PART 1 - THE EARLY YEARS

Playing snooker on my 6x3 table in my parents back living room watching the snooker on the tv at the same time. i would be semi thinking that i was actually there at the crucible playing whilst potting the balls on this little table. Also beating my bad tempered friend so much that he would storm out of the house slamming the door behind him. As for my school days well they consisted of getting home as quickly as i could to get changed so i could get up the local snooker club in Tavistock by 5pm to play for about 2 hours.Very thankfull to my dad for giving me the money in order to play most nights. This was the game for me as i lived every minute and day for. My dad used to drive me on in every way to the point where we would talk about snooker all the time so much so that my sister got so fedup that she wrote a poem about TALKING SNOOKER. and then putting it up on the living room wall. There was one morning when i got up for school got changed went down for quick breakfast and then picked up my school bag and rushed out the door. I was about 50 yards down the street when suddenly i realised that it wasnt my bag. It was my snooker cue. Totally true. Little things i remember are making my first 40 break against Derek Walker who coached me from the age of 12 to 15 great bloke who still loves the game and is currenty running the Belgrave snooke club in Plymouth where i sometimes practice. I then just turned 13 when making my first hundred break 101 against Ian Cook from what was called the pot black snooker club in Plymouth. Also making my first 147 at the age of 15 and one month. And when getting the bus home i was so excited to tell my dad that when i was running from the bus down my street to get to the house i remember a dog running out of a house and attacked my leg which frightened the life out of me. When i got home i told my dad and he said to me well done im going to ring up the local papers to let them know. So i replied why. And dad said to me. Listen son making a 147 is a great achievment and you might not ever make another one for the rest of you life. Well you can imagine my look of horror when replying DONT BE SO STUPID OF COURSE I''LL MAKE ANOTHER ONO. Needless to say it took me only 3 weeks to make my second.And what with now the last count which is 285 maximums i think iv made my point to my dad bless him. Dad used to take me down every wednesday evening to the pot black club where i would play Andy Snell who was probably in the top ten of the amateur rankings at the time. Great great player who was slow and methodical. We would play 6 til 8 and manage to get about 6 frames in if we were lucky where he would beat me 5 to 6 nil every week with out fail. I would get so down hearted loosing week after week whilst my dad sleeping in the chair by about 6.45 when being tiered from working all day. But gradually over time i would start pinching the odd frame or two until finally one evening i managed to beat him 5.2. Im telling you it felt like i had just won the world championship.

PART 2 - THE EARLY YEARS

Achievment days as an amatuer Here are some of the tournaments that meant something to me as an amatuer.

1. Firstly winning the turkey trot open in Tavistock when i was 12 years old. The feeling of holding this turkey above my head which incidently i could barely lift was amazing.It was like i was the best player in the world.
2. Winning the pontins star of the future at 14 years old.I was in great company winning this as the likes of Ronnie O Sullivan Mark Williams Matthew Stevens And Paul Hunter all have there name on this trophy.
3. Winning the Plymouth And District Individual five years in a row from the age of 13 to 17 and then turning pro.
4. And then what i class as my best achievment was winning the British Under 19s at the age of 17 beating John Higgins on the way in the last 8.

Well thats enough of blowing my own trumpet.All i can say is they were great times looking back where snooker seemed to be so much of my life as a kid. I remember at the age of 15 i was taking my exams when i had to make a choice weather to play the last of the three major amatuer tournaments where i was ranked at about 35 after the first two.This would mean missing two of my final exams at school. But after the tournament if i were to get into the top 28 i could have turned pro at the age of 15.Anyway i did miss my exams which i am not proud of but thats how much snooker meant to me. I went and lost first round and didnt qualify to be pro that year. Images Courtesy of: Andy Hicks & Richard Pearson

BUSINESS CARD

