  ('New Blazey boys'' perfect start', 'new-blazey-boys-perfect-start', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Last year’s runners-up Tywardreath ‘A’ now playing as St Blazey ‘B’ showed they mean business this year in their quest to become champions as they returned with a nap hand from Bugle ‘B’ with Mike Hancock’s 30 and…', null, '"Defending champions Bugle ‘A’ saw off newcomers St Blazey ‘A’ 4-1, with Chris Brown defeating Andy Peers to save the whitewash."', 'Last year’s runners-up Tywardreath ‘A’ now playing as St Blazey ‘B’ showed they mean business this year in their quest to become champions as they returned with a nap hand from Bugle ‘B’ with Mike Hancock’s 30 and 67, Paul Baron’s 35 and Andrew Gooch’s 52 dominating the night.

more Defending champions Bugle ‘A’ saw off newcomers St Blazey ‘A’ 4-1, with Chris Brown defeating Andy Peers to save the whitewash. Other newcomers to the top league Bethel ‘D’ crashed 4-1 to their ‘A’ side with Ryan Tonkin the solitary winner as Leon Collard’s 38-break sealed the win for the away side. St Neot opened up with a 3-2 home victory over Mevagissey ‘A’ with 13-year-old Callum Lundon seeing off veteran Gerald Goode in the decider. The Lostwithiel derby went in favour of the ‘A’ side 4-1 with George Lory playing very well to secure the ‘B’ team’ point. Newcomers St Stephen opened their Rees account by defeating Gorran Haven 3-2 with Matt Fox and Lee McInstry on target for the seasiders. Rob Blamey’s last frame win sealed a derby victory for Mevagissey ‘D’ over their ‘C’ side whilst on the other table relegated Mevagissey ‘B’ scored a noteable 3-2 win over Bodmin Sparks as both teams are sure to be in the running for promotion this year. The Bethel derby went the way of the ‘B’ side as Guy Buckle, Matt Green and the returning Jim Tamblyn secured a fine win. Paul Thomas and ‘potting machine’ Roger Smithson won for the home side.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Bethel ‘D’ 1 (R Tonkin) Bethel ‘A’ 4 (G Stephens, G Hawken, L Collard (38), B Stephens), Bugle ‘B’ 0 St Blazey ‘B’ 5 (M Hancock (67+30), A Gooch (52), P Baron (35), G Taylor, D Kirwan), Lostwithiel ‘A’ 4 (D Courtis, C Perring, D Lock (43), P Ormand) Lostwithiel ‘B’ 1 (G Lory), St Blazey ‘A’ 1 (C Brown) Bugle ‘A’ 4 (P Sturtridge (31), A Best, G Spencer, I Powell), St Neot 3 (S Courts, T Farrant, C Lundon) Mevagissey ‘A’ 2 (M Mills, L Varcoe). Rees Memorial League: Bethel ‘C’ 2 (P Thomas, R Smithson) Bethel ‘B’ 3 (J Tamblyn, M Green, G Buckle), Mevagissey ‘B’ 3 (B Rothwell, M Gregory, D Kendall) Sparks 2 (C Stuthridge, M Williams), Mevagissey ‘C’ 2 (M Chesterfield, B Prynn) Mevagissey ‘D’ 3 (R Hurrell, T Blamey, R Blamey), St Stephen 3 (A Harrison, J Bennett, C Clark) Gorran Haven 2 (M Fox, L McKinstry). Top breaks of the week: M Hancock 67, 30, A Gooch 52, P Baron 35, D Lock 43, P Sturtridge 31 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('New leaders at the top of the Victory League', 'new-leaders-at-the-top-of-the-victory-league', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Mevagissey ‘A’ have taken over at the top of the Victory League after thrashing Bethel ‘A’ 4-1.', null, 'Pelynt and Bugle ‘B’ fought out a tight affair with Richard Broad pipping Mark Sturtridge in the first and Phil Ormand putting them 2-0 up, Dave Rundle replied.', 'Mevagissey ‘A’ have taken over at the top of the Victory League after thrashing Bethel ‘A’ 4-1.

more Matt Green’s blackball win over Phil Staley got the away side off to a good start, but Matt Mills defeated Leon ‘The Hatman’ in the next and further wins for James Popplewell, Leighton Varcoe and Paul Williams, who knocked in a 54 break, lifted the Seasiders to the summit. Tregonissey ‘A’ took four points at home to Mevagissey ‘B’ through Ryan Bilson, Barrie McIntosh (30 break), Jason Cocks and Rich Pearson – with Ben Rothwell knocked in a 30 whilst defeating Martin Knight for Meva’s welcome point. Tregonissey ‘B’ picked up two good points at St Blazey through Mike Hooley and Andrew Gooch with Simon Garrington having a 31 break when losing to Chris Morford, Liam Reynolds and Mickey Meares got the home side’s other points to lift them to fourth. Mevagissey ‘D’ got a great win at St Neot. They are finding their feet in the top league and wins for Scott Mills, Ian Latham and Mark Ashton secured the win with Darren Lock and Steve Courts, who knocked in a 48, getting the points. Pelynt and Bugle ‘B’ fought out a tight affair with Richard Broad pipping Mark Sturtridge in the first and Phil Ormand putting them 2-0 up, Dave Rundle replied. But ‘Mr Consistent’ Andy Parnell got the win with Keith Moore hammering Lenny Flowers in the last to get Bugle’s second point. Rees League leaders Mevagissey ‘C’ had a night off so it allowed St Stephen to close the gap with a brilliant 4-1 win at Bethel ‘D’. Anthony Harrison, Anthony Garside and Chris Brown, and a walkover got St Stephen’s points, with Jason Pascoe replying for Bethel. Lostwithiel picked up a good win at Gorran, through Mark McGurrell, Josh Doherty and Terry Nicholls with Rob Bunney continuing his unbeaten run and back from his holiday, Lee McKinstry, getting the home points. Bethel ‘C’ raced into a 3-0 lead at Bugle ‘C’ but Roy Bayliss and Damien Knight got the last two frames to put a bit of respectability to the result. Bethel ‘C’ and Treggy ‘C’ is to be played on November 22.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Mevagissey ‘A’ 4 (M Mills, J Popplewell, L Varcoe, P Williams (59)) Bethel ‘A’ 1 (M Green), Tregonissey ‘A’ 4 (R Bilson, B McIntosh (30), J Cocks, R Pearson), Mevagissey ‘B’ 1 (B Rothwell (30)), Pelynt 3 (R Broad, P Ormand, A Parnell) Bugle ‘B’ 2 (D Rundle, K Moore), St Blazey 3 (C Morford, L Reynolds, M Meares) Tregonissey ‘B’ 2 (M Hooley, A Gooch), St Neot 2 (D Lock, S Courts (48)) Mevagissey ‘D’ 3 (S Mills, I Latham, M Ashton). Rees Memorial League: Gorran 2 (R Bunney, L McKinstry) Lostwithiel 3 (M McGurrell, J Doherty, T Nicholls), Bugle ‘C’ 2 (R Bayliss, D Knight) Bethel ‘B’ 3 (C Stuthridge, R Bolt, M Isted), Bethel ‘D’ 1 (J Pascoe) St Stephen 4 (A Harrison, A Garside, C Brown, D Polmounter). Top breaks of the week: P Williams 59, B McIntosh 30, B Rothwell 30 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('New leaders in the Victory League', 'new-leaders-in-the-victory-league-2', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Tregonissey ‘A’ have jumped back to the top of the St Blazey and District Snooker Victory league after completing a full house at Bugle ‘B’.', null, 'Gorran also picked up a great result beating Bethel ‘B’ 3-2. Andy Reddall’s win over Jim Tamblyn got them going with Lee McKinstry and unbeaten Rob Bunney adding to their total with Clive Stuthridge and Richie Bolt replying.', 'Tregonissey ‘A’ have jumped back to the top of the St Blazey and District Snooker Victory league after completing a full house at Bugle ‘B’.

more There were wins for Jason Simmons, Barrie McIntosh, who knocked in a 40 break, Martin Knight, Ryan Bilson and Jason Cocks. Bugle ‘A’ move up to second place after a 3-2 win at Mevagissey ‘D’. Dave Kendall defeated Jake Moore and Ben Rothwell beat Paul Sturtridge for the home points with Nathan shaw just losing out to Andrew Best with Ian Ball and Gary Spencer clinching the win. Bethel ‘A’ got back on track after two heavy defeats with a 3-2 win over St Neot. Geoff Hawken and Leon Collard’s blackball win over Chris Perring put them two up but Darren Lock knocked in breaks of 31 and 36 defeating Andy Pears – but Matt Green’s win over an out of sorts Steve Courts sealed Bethel’s win with Andy Pearce hammering Garry Stephens with a great potting display to get St Neot’s second point. Mike and Phil Hooley got Tregonissey ‘B’s points in a 3-2 home defeat to Pelynt, who replied through Richard Broad, Phil Ormond and Len Flower. St Blazey remain fourth after a 3-2 win at Mevagissey ‘D’. Nick Kendall, Chris Morford and Mickey Meares got the away points with Rich Lakeman and Mark Ahston replying. The ‘Predator’ Tregale is flying and he’s already been out and bought the brasso after a recent Bill Toms victory and his team Mevagissey ‘C’ getting a 5-0 win at Tregonissey ‘C’ to keep them clear at the top of the Rees Legue. Ben Prynn, Adrian Evans along with Joe and Matt Chesterfield keep them rolling along. Bethel ‘C’ picked up a brilliant win at St Stephen with Roger Smithson first winning with Ben Stunnell, Leigh Rosevear and Paul Thomas also securing victory as Chris Brown replied. Gorran also picked up a great result beating Bethel ‘B’ 3-2. Andy Reddall’s win over Jim Tamblyn got them going with Lee McKinstry and unbeaten Rob Bunney adding to their total with Clive Stuthridge and Richie Bolt replying. Dad and son Terry and Craig Nicholls both won but Lostwithiel went down 3-2 to Bethel ‘D’ as Jason Pascoe, Gavin Dyche and Ross McMenemy clinching the win. And finally Lerryn continued to pick up points as Charles McMiram, Graham Hoskins and Matt Woodward secured victory over Bugle ‘C’ who had Roy Bayliss and Mo Roscorla getting their points. The latest draw for the league competitions was made by Rob Bunney and Andy Pearce at Bethel on Tuesday and have been distributed to all clubs and can be viewed on the league website.

FULL RESULTS

Victory League: Mevagissey ‘B’ 2 (D Kendall, B Rothwell) Bugle ‘A’ 3 (I Ball, A Best, G Spencer), Bugle ‘B’ 0 Tregonissey ‘A’ 5 (J Simmons, B McIntosh (40), M Knight, R Bilson, J Cocks), Tregonissey ‘B’ 2 (M Hooley, P Hooley) Pelynt 3 (R Broad, P Ormand, K Flower), Mevagissey ‘D’ 2 (R Lakeman, M Ashton) St Blazey 3 (N Kendall, C Morford, M Meares), Bethel ‘A’ 3 (G Hawken, L Collard, M Green) St Neot 2 (D Lock (36+31), A Pearce). Rees Memorial League: Bethel ‘B’ 2 (C Stuthridge, R Bolt) Gorran 3 (A Reddall, L McKinstry, R Bunney), Lerryn 3 (C McMiram, G Hoskins, M Woodward) Bugle ‘C’ 2 (R Bayliss, M Roscorla), Tregonissey ‘C’ 0 Mevagissey ‘C’ 5 (D Tregale, B Prynn, A Evans, J Chesterfield, M Chesterfield), St Stephen 1 (C Brown) Bethel ‘C’ 4 (R Smithson, B Stunnell, L Rosevear, P Thomas), Lostwithiel 2 (C Nicholls, T Nicholls) Bethel ‘D’ 3 (J Pascoe, G Dyche, R McMenemy). Top breaks of the week: B McIntosh 40, D Lock 36+31 Additional Information League Tables Rankings Competitions Breaks Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('New player or team online entry form', 'new-player-or-team-online-entry-form', 'League News', '[]'::jsonb, 'If you wish to enter a new player or team, then please fill in the online form to be considered', null, null, 'If you wish to enter a new player or team, then please fill in the online form to be considered', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('No scare for Bugle on Halloween', 'no-scare-for-bugle-on-halloween', 'Match Reports', '[]'::jsonb, 'Bugle ‘A’ have moved to the top of the Victory League after thrashing Bethel A 4-1 which was played on Monday night due to Bugle’s Halloween commitments.', null, 'Mevagissey B recovered from last weeks hammering by defeating Pelynt 3-2. It started well for the away side as Richard Broad beat Nathan Shaw. Mark Gregory and Matt Rowe put Meva ahaead but Andy Parnell beat Ben Rothwell to level. It was Dave Kendall who saw off Phil Ormond with an excellent display to seal the win.', 'Bugle ‘A’ have moved to the top of the Victory League after thrashing Bethel A 4-1 which was played on Monday night due to Bugle’s Halloween commitments.

more Gary Spencer knocked in a a fine 41 break when seeing off Matt Green. Wins followed for Ian Ball and Jake Moore with Andrew Best having a 36 when defeating Andy Peers. Geoff Hawken breezed past Paul Sturtridge for the home point. Mevagissey D took out their A team 3-2 to snatch their first win. Mark Ashton and Paul Williams traded frames with the D team, going ahead through Ian Latham’s re-spot black win over Phil Staley The win was confirmed when Rich Lakeman knocked in a 30 to defeat Matt Mills. James Popplewell also had a 30 break beating Rob Blamey for the A teams second point. Mevagissey B recovered from last weeks hammering by defeating Pelynt 3-2. It started well for the away side as Richard Broad beat Nathan Shaw. Mark Gregory and Matt Rowe put Meva ahaead but Andy Parnell beat Ben Rothwell to level. It was Dave Kendall who saw off Phil Ormond with an excellent display to seal the win. St Blazey picked up a useful win at Bugle B through Liam Reynolds, Nick Kendall and Chris Morford with Colin and Dave Rundle replying for Bugle. Tregonissey B and St Neot was postponed Mevagissey C march on at the top of the Rees League after their fifth win of the season, this time at Lerryn. Matt and Joe Chesterfield alongside ‘Hellfire’ Evans secured the victory with Terry Middleton pipping Ben Prynn by a point and Matt Woodward’s win over ‘Predator’ Tregale getting the home points. Bugle C picked up four good points at Lostwithiel. Phil Johns, Roy Bayliss, Mo Rescorla and Kevin Spencer took frames which Bugle up to mid-table with games in hand over all above. Craig Nicholls replied for the home side. Chris Brown knocked in a cracking 35 break as St Stephen overcame Gorran 3-2. Ady Bowyer and Anthony Garside secured the other home points with Mike Towsey and Andy Reddall replying. Bethel D with a 4-1 win at Tregonissey C. Lee Searle, Dave Morshead, Jason Pascoe and Dayle Dyche winning. Amanda Fleming got the home point with a victory over Ross McMenemy. Images Courtesy of: Richard Pearson', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('Nugget Set For Jungle Adventure', 'nugget-set-for-jungle-adventure', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Six-time World Champion Steve Davis faces one of the biggest challenges of his life, having arrived in the Australian out-back for the ITV show ''I''m A Celebrity...Get Me Out Of Here.''', null, '""It may be harder than winning a snooker final purely because it is not what I was cut out to do. Of course I want to still play pro snooker for as long as possible but occasionally it is nice to enjoy myself too. Fingers crossed, I do represent myself and snooker in a good light whilst in the jungle and be as good value as I can be."""', 'Six-time World Champion Steve Davis faces one of the biggest challenges of his life, having arrived in the Australian out-back for the ITV show ''I''m A Celebrity...Get Me Out Of Here.''

more The green baize legend will spend much of the next month living in a jungle with nine other celebs, including Olympic gold medallist Rebecca Adlington and Alfonso Ribeiro, aka Carlton from The Fresh Prince Of Bel Air. Bush-tucker trials, creepy crawlies, sleeping in a hammock and surviving the public votes...56-year-old Steve has got it all to come over the next four weeks. "Many people lead comfy lives these days so a little bit of deprivation is perhaps good for the soul," he said. "Like many viewers, I would like to know, faced with a plate of animal innards or a cockroach filled space helmet, whether I could handle it. "And now as it is getting closer, I''m feeling more excited but the trepidation Richter scale is rising fast! I''m not really using the jungle as any type of ''stepping stone'' but purely just to have a laugh and a challenge. "I''ve had my 15 minutes of fame. I''d swap the fame and jungle notoriety for another World Championship at the drop of a cork ridden hat but I''m further down the pecking order and bookmaker lists in snooker than I am even in being crowned King of the jungle. "There seems to be a growing opinion that I''m not as boring as people once thought and it''s my aim while I am in the jungle to put a stop to this once and for all. And if anyone wants any snooker coaching then I''m their man. "I am going to miss not being able to contact my girlfriend, Jeannie, and my two sons, Greg and Jack, although I think they will both refuse to say they know me while the show is being aired. "I am not sure how I am going to cope with the lack of food. But I am not a massive carbohydrate and sugar addict and so hopefully I will be better than most. My biggest fear actually is not being able to stop swearing. "It may be harder than winning a snooker final purely because it is not what I was cut out to do. Of course I want to still play pro snooker for as long as possible but occasionally it is nice to enjoy myself too. Fingers crossed, I do represent myself and snooker in a good light whilst in the jungle and be as good value as I can be." So will he be discussing the miss rule with Amy Willerton, talking Sugar Hut escapades with Joey Essex or considering the qualities of a brass ferrule with Laila Morse? Make sure you find out by tuning into the show, which starts on Sunday at 9pm. We''ll be following Steve''s progress closely with a blog on this website and on Twitter (follow @WorldSnooker1) using the hashtag #TheNugget So join in the jungle fun and make sure you vote to keep Steve Down Under...until the bitter end! Article courtesy of the World Snooker Images Courtesy of: World Snooker', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson'),
  ('O''Sullivan Beats Murphy To Equal Titles Record', 'osullivan-beats-murphy-to-equal-titles-record', 'Professional Snooker', '[]'::jsonb, 'Ronnie O’Sullivan won his fifth ranking title of 2017/18 – equalling the record for the most in a single season – by beating Shaun Murphy 10-4 in the final of the Ladbrokes Players Championship.', null, '“The crowds here in Llandudno have been fantastic. This is one of our best tournaments and I hope it stays here.”', 'Ronnie O’Sullivan won his fifth ranking title of 2017/18 – equalling the record for the most in a single season – by beating Shaun Murphy 10-4 in the final of the Ladbrokes Players Championship.

more O’Sullivan was not at his best in the final in Llandudno, failing to score a single century break, but made several crucial clearances, winning five frames from at least 23 points behind, and that swung the contest in his favour. Having already landed the English Open, Shanghai Masters, UK Championship and Ladbrokes World Grand Prix crowns this season, O’Sullivan matches the record of five in a single campaign, set by Stephen Hendry in 1990/91 and equalled by Ding Junhui in 2013/14 and Mark Selby in 2016/17. O’Sullivan has played in ten ranking events this season, winning half of them, and has taken the title every time he has reached the semi-final stage. The £125,000 top prize takes O’Sullivan’s earnings for 2017/18 to £798,500, the second highest ever in one season, with Selby’s record of £932,000 within his sights. Indeed O’Sullivan could break the £1 million barrier by winning next month’s China Open even before he heads to the World Championship. The 42-year-old’s career total of ranking titles now stands at 33, just three behind Hendry’s record of 36. In his current form and frame of mind, O’Sullivan could surpass all of Hendry’s records by the time he retires. He remains second in the world rankings but closes the gap on first place, and could go to the Crucible next month with a chance to take the top spot from Mark Selby, who has held it for over three years. Murphy has now lost all four of the ranking finals he has contested this season including the UK Championship where he lost 10-5 to O’Sullivan, though he did win the invitational Champion of Champions with a 10-8 defeat of the Rocket. O’Sullivan led 6-3 after the first session and extended his advantage in the opening frame tonight, coming from 43-0 down to take it with a 92 clearance. Murphy pulled one back with a superb 137 total clearance and he had a scoring chance in the next frame but missed the blue to a baulk corner when trailing 40-7, and O’Sullivan made 54 to go 8-4 ahead. Again in frame 13 Murphy missed a ball at a key moment – a red to a centre pocket – and again O’Sullivan punished him as his run of 76 made it 9-4 at the interval. Murphy also had first scoring opportunity in the 14th but could only make 23, and O’Sullivan’s 85 took him past the winning post. “Shaun didn’t play as well today as he did in the semi-finals which was a shame for the final,” said O’Sullivan. “I kept the pressure on and played decent snooker. In a lot of the frames he got in first but he kept breaking down on 40 or 50 and I made some good clearances. I did what I had to do although I didn’t have my scoring boots on. I feel quite drained because I put a lot of effort into the match. “I have played better in other seasons but not won as many titles. I am pacing myself in terms of which tournaments I play in and travel to, and I have cut down on practice in order to just play when I feel like it. It seems to be working at the moment.” Asked about chasing Hendry’s record of 36 ranking titles, O’Sullivan responded: “I think it is well achievable to beat it. I always thought that record was more realistic for me than the world titles (he has five to Hendry’s seven). There are still a few records to go for. I think I will pass on winning seven or eight world titles. To get to 1,000 centuries would be a good one and to win 37 ranking events would be nice. “It would be great to win the world title but I’ll just go out there and play on my terms and if it comes, it comes. I’ve had a fantastic season anyway. If I play my best game then it’s probably only John Higgins and Mark Selby who can play at the same level. “The crowds here in Llandudno have been fantastic. This is one of our best tournaments and I hope it stays here.” Murphy, who has been affected by an injury which causes pain in his back and neck for the past few weeks, said: “To come here with no preparation and to have had a chance to take the trophy home on the last night against the best player ever – I have to count the week as a success. Obviously I would much rather have the trophy than the runner-up medal. I have had a very consistent season and there are two massive events to go. “It was frustrating today because I have had matches against Ronnie where I have been swamped, but today I had chances to win almost every frame. Somehow I managed to lose a lot of frames I should have won. In the end I simply wasn’t clinical enough. Scrapping it out in the trenches when the balls are messy and the colours are safe is still the weakness in my game. My general safety has improved and my scoring has always been good. But in those scrappy frames I’m not as good as the likes of Mark Selby. “Without doubt Ronnie can win more than 36 ranking titles. Stephen Hendry will probably go home and try to work out which records he still holds, because he might not have them for much longer! When Ronnie is in the right frame of mind he is the best. “I’m looking forward to the China Open now although the journey won’t do me any favours and I’ll need to have some physiotherapy in the days leading up to it. Then looking ahead to the World Championship, I think if I keep my form going I might have a decent run in Sheffield.” Article & Images courtesy of the World Snooker', '2014-01-01', false, 'Richard Pearson');
